2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://www.rootsweb.com/~lastchar/landry.htm>:
2 CONT
2 CONT LANDRY FAMILY
2 CONT
2 CONT The Acadian Landry family is originally from La Ventrouze, near Mortagne-au-Perche in the modern French department of l'Orne. Jean-Claude Landry came to Acadia in 1640 or 1641 with his second wife, Marie Sallée and their son, René
2 CONC  Landry dit LeJeune (the younger), who was born in 1634 in France. They were also accompanied by three of Jean-Claude's adult children from his first marriage with a woman whose name remains unknown. One of these three children, P
2 CONC errine Landry was accompanied by her husband, Jacques Joffriau. The other two were fraternal twins, René Landry dit l'Aisné (the elder) and Anthoinette Landry. The twins were 22 years old and unmarried at the time of their arrival
2 CONC . The family was also accompanied by three children of Marie Sallé from her first marriage to the deceased Martin Aucoin. The family was likely attracted to Acadia by another daughter of Jean-Claude, Marguerite, who was married to
2 CONC  Robert Martin and living in Acadia for several years prior to 1640.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anthoinette Landry married Anthoine Bourg in 1641, shortly after the family's arrival in Acadia. Her twin brother married in 1645 to Perrine Bourg, the widow of the late Simon Pelletret. Perrine brought four young children to the 
2 CONC marriage, three girls and one boy. She was only nineteen years old at the time of her second marriage. She had married Simon Pelletret when she was fourteen years old. Perrine Bourg was born in Poitou province of France at Loudun,
2 CONC  one of the principal sites of origin for Acadian families.
2 CONT
2 CONT René Landry dit l'Aisné and Perrine Bourg had five children, three girls and two boys. The girls were Marie dit l'Aisné (b. 1646 Port-Royal, m. 1664 Germain Doucet), Marie dit la Cadette (b. 1648 Port-Royal, m. 1-1667 Laurent Gran
2 CONC ger, m. 2-Estienne Comeau), and Magdelaine (b. 1656 P-R, m. 1-1676 René Richard dit Beaupré, m. 2-1692 Pierre Dupuis). Interestingly, the first husband of Marie dit La Cadette was an English sailor and he and his family became one
2 CONC  of several non-French persons who assimilated into French Acadian society over the years.
2 CONT
2 CONT René Landry and Perrine Bourg's boys were named Pierre (b. 1658 P-R, m.1682 Magdeleine Robichaud, the daughter of Estienne Robichaud and Françoise Boudrot) and Claude (b. 1663 P- R, m. 1683 Marguerite Therriot, the daughter of Cla
2 CONC ude Therriot and Marie Gautherot (Gautreaux).
2 CONT
2 CONT Perrine Bourg's children, from both of her marriages, were quite prolific, and before her death, she was surrounded by 73 direct descendants. Because of this, she is sometimes called the grandmother of Acadie.
2 CONT
2 CONT The younger half-brother of Rene dit l'Aisné, also named René but with the nickname, LeJeune, married Marie Bernard, the daughter of André Bernard and Andrée Guyon. They had fifteen children, eight boys and seven girls. The eight 
2 CONC boys were Anthoine (b. 1661 P-R, m. 1682 Marie Thibodeau, the daughter of Pierre Thibodeau and Jeanne Therriot), Claude (b. 1663 P-R, m. 1- 1684 Marie-Catherine Thibodeau, the sister of Anthoine's wife, Marie, m. 2-1725 Marie Babi
2 CONC n, the daughter of Anthoine Babin and Marie Mercier, m. 3-Jeanne Bellemaire, widow of Mathieu Le Brasseur and daughter of André Bellemaire and Perrine Bazille), Jehan (b. 1666 P-R, m. 1687 Cécile Melanson, the daughter of Pierre M
2 CONC elanson and Marguerite Mius d'Entremont), René (b. 1668 P-R, m. 1691 Anne Therriot, the daughter of Claude Therriot and Marie Gautherot), Germain (b. 1674 P-R, m. 1694 Marie Melanson, daughter of Pierre Melanson and Marguerite Miu
2 CONC s d'Entremont), Abraham (b. 1678 P-R, m. Marie Guillebault (Guilbeau), the daughter of Pierre Guillebault and Catherine Therriot), Pierre (b. 1680 P-R, m. 1704 Magdelaine Brossard (Broussard), the daughter of François Brossard and
2 CONC  Catherine Richard) and Charles (b. 1682 P-R, m. Catherine Brossard, the sister of Magdelaine Brossard).
2 CONT
2 CONT All of Jean-Claude Landry's grandchildren were born in or around Port-Royal, however by the 1670s that region that included the small town and the farming settlements along the Rivière des Dauphins was getting quite crowded. Avail
2 CONC able farm land was becoming scarce, and for that reason several Acadian families began looking to the east for more land to cultivate. Pierre Melanson was already established on the banks of the Bassin des Mines (Minas Basin) abou
2 CONC t 60 miles east of Port-Royal. In late summer 1680, after the harvest, a group of Acadians including two sons of René Landry dit LeJeune, Anthoine and Claude, moved east to join Pierre Melanson on the Bassin des Mines. The group i
2 CONC ncluded Pierre Therriot, who would later become the brother-in-law of Anthoine and Claude. The group first built a house for Pierre Therriot, that served as shelter for the entire group through the first winter. They then began cl
2 CONC earing high ground through the autumn and winter in order to construct homesites for the others. In the spring, they returned to the Port-Royal region to plant and prepare the crop. After the next harvest, the group returned to th
2 CONC e Bassin to continue the preparatory work for the new settlement.
2 CONT
2 CONT There was much to be done. Homes had to be constructed on the high ground. Paul Surette, an Acadian historian from Moncton, New Brunswick has documented that Acadian settlers sought a homesite on the southern flank of a hill or ri
2 CONC dge to protect the home from the cold winter's wind. Once the high homesite was prepared, it was time to prepare the fields for cultivation. Rather than clear forest land for cultivation, the Acadians preferred the very fertile lo
2 CONC w lands along the numerous rivers in the region. However, the Bassin des Mines has the distinction of being the region of the world with the highest natural sea tides. Therefore, high tides would daily inundate the very land that 
2 CONC the Acadians wanted to farm! In order to solve this problem, the Acadians adapted a technique they had learned in Europe of building dykes with a system of water gates to allow rainwater to drain to the sea, but that did not allow
2 CONC  water from the sea to come onto the land. This system of dykes was called the aboiteaux. Some of the original aboiteaux built by Acadian settlers in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries are still standing and workable. It too
2 CONC k two or three years once the aboiteaux were finished for the land to become sufficiently desalinized to be able to be cultivated. But the result was some of the most fertile farm land in North America.
2 CONT
2 CONT The work done by this small group of settlers was the foundation of the settlement of Grand Pré, which later became famous as a principal point of embarkation of Acadian settlers during the deportation in 1755.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anthoine and Claude Landry eventually settled at Bassin des Mines with their families on the banks of the Riviére-des-Habitants. The other married brothers and sisters of Anthoine and Claude followed them to the region. Jehan, Céc
2 CONC ile and Marguerite established themselves at Grand Pré. Marie and her family settled at Riviére-aux-Gaspereux. Jeanne and her family settled at Riviére de Pigiguit.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first Landrys arrived in Louisiana in the mid-1760s. Four of the first Landry arrivals were settled along the Mississippi River by 1766. It is possible that they arrived in 1765 from Halifax with a group of Acadians who escape
2 CONC d the original deportations in 1755 but who were later captured by the British and held prisoner. This group of Acadians were some of the few who arrived from Louisiana directly from old Acadia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Most of the first Landrys to arrive in Louisiana came from Maryland, however. They had been deported in 1755 to that American colony, and like all of the Acadians deported to the eastern seabord colonies suffered persecution and m
2 CONC isery for a decade before learning of the Spanish colony of Louisiana that welcomed them. Among the indignities suffered at the hands of the Americans in Massachusetts was the forced removal of Acadian children from their families
2 CONC  to be placed as indentured servants with American families. Eventually, a group of Massachusetts Acadians, including a number of Landry families, managed to leave and go to Québec. Among those who managed to find refuge in Québec
2 CONC  was the family of François-Joseph Landry and his adult children and grandchildren. François-Joseph was the son of Anthoine Landry, one of the founders of the Acadian settlements on the Bassin des Mines.
2 CONT
2 CONT At least nine Landry families arrived in Louisiana from Maryland in 1767 and five more families followed them in 1768. These 14 families settled either at St. Gabriel in present day Iberville Parish or at Ascension de Lafourche de
2 CONC s Chetimaches (present day Donaldsonville). Ascension Parish and the northern stretch of Bayou Lafourche were thus the center of the Landry clan in Louisiana during the late eighteenth and early nineteenth century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Landry (m. Marie-Josephe Bourg) established a prominent line of the Landry family in Ascension Parish. His son, Joseph, dit Belhomme Landry (m. 1775 Isabelle LeBlanc, m. 1779 Anne Bujol) was named commandant of the Acadian 
2 CONC militia in Ascension Parish. He and his six sons developed a large indigo plantation, Nouvel Espoir, near Donaldsonville. Joseph dit Belhomme's youngest son, Jean-Trasimond (m. 1825 Modeste Breaux, widow of his brother, Achilles),
2 CONC  was elected Lieutenant governor of Louisiana in 1845. Another of Belhomme's sons, Narcisse (m. 1807 Henriette Blanchard, m. 1829 Marie Comes) had two large plantations near Donaldsonville. These families were largely ruined finan
2 CONC cially by the Civil War and its aftermath.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another settler by way of Maryland was Abraham Landry (m. 1732 Élisabeth Leblanc). His six sons and their descendants were small farmers on the Mississippi River. Alexandre Landry (m. 1732 Anne Flan) arrived from Maryland, and he 
2 CONC and his five sons settled near St. Gabriel. The west bank of the Mississippi, in present day West Baton Rouge Parish was settled by two sons of Augustin Landry (m. Marie Babin), Joseph-Ignace (m. 1776 Scolastique Breaux, m. 1787 I
2 CONC sabelle Breaux) and Mathurin (m. 1779 Perpétue Breaux, m. 1800 Marie Hébert).
2 CONT
2 CONT Several of the families who came from Maryland and originally settled along the Mississippi River eventually moved west into the Teche country. The first to make this move was Firmin Landry (m. 1752 ??lisabeth Thibodeau, m. 1776 T
2 CONC héotiste Thibodeaux), a widower, who was established on the upper Bayou Vermilion by 1770 with two grown sons, Joseph and Saturin. He eventually moved south to a tract on Bayou Teche at Fausse Pointe. His son, Joseph (m. 1775 Mari
2 CONC e-Anne Melançon) acquired land on Bayou Vermilion. Some of his descendants remained in the Lafayette area, while others moved to Vermilion Parish. Two others of Firmin's sons by his second marriage, Hubert (m. 1800 Euphrosine Legr
2 CONC os) and Valentin (m. 1801 Marie Hébert) settled near their father at Fausse Pointe. Most of their descendants remained on the southern stretch of the Teche below New Iberia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other Maryland exiles to settle the Attakapas were two sons of René Landry (m. 1737 Marie Terriot, m. 1753 Anne Landry), Olivier (m. 1775 Marie-Madeleine Hébert) and Joseph (m. 1789 Marie-Rose Melançon). They settled on the Côte G
2 CONC elée, near the present day town of Broussard in Lafayette Parish. One of Joseph's sons, Onésime (m. 1827 Carmélite Landry), moved to Vermilion Parish.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ten Landry families arrived in Louisiana in 1785 with the group of Acadians who came from Nantes, France. One of these families was headed by Prosper Landry and his third wife, Élisabeth Pitre (m. 1761, m. 1751 Anne-Josette Boudro
2 CONC t, m. 1754 Marie-Josephe Bourg). Prosper Landry arrived at New Orleans from Nantes aboard the ship, La Bergère on August 15, 1785. In addition to his wife, he was accompanied by two sons, Jean-Pierre and Simon-Joseph. Prosper Land
2 CONC ry was the son of Jean-Baptiste Landry and Marguérite Comeau. Jean-Baptiste, in turn, was the son of Claude Landry and Catherine Thibodeau and the grandson of René dit Lejeune Landry and Marie Bernard of Port-Royal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prosper Landry and his sons settled on Bayou Lafourche. His son, Jean-Pierre was married twice, first to Isabelle Guérin and then to Anne Daigle. Simon-Joseph Landry was born in France in 1766 and came to Louisiana at the age of 1
2 CONC 9. He was among a large group of Acadian children born during the years of exile who never saw Acadia. Simon-Joseph (m. 1795 Marie Bourque) inherited his father's farm and farmed next door to his brother on Bayou Lafourche. At lea
2 CONC st one of Simon-Joseph's grandsons, Octave (m. Marie-Angéline Giroir) moved to the Youngsville area of Lafayette Parish during the 1860s. Some of his children later acquired land near the Leroy settlement in Vermilion Parish.
2 CONT
2 CONT Today, the Landry family is one of the largest Acadian families in Louisiana, along with the Hébert, Broussard and LeBlanc families. There are concentrations of Landrys in all parts of south Louisiana, but they are especially cent
2 CONC ered in the Baton Rouge, Lafayette and New Iberia regions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Many thanks to Mr. Ray Landry of Lafayette, the great-great-great gandson of Simon-Joseph Landry and Marie Bourque, for some of the information included in this article. The Landry family reunion during Congrès Mondial Acadien-Lou
2 CONC isiane 1999 will be held August 13-15, 1999 at Grand Coteau, Louisiana. You may contact Frances Landry at (318) 937-5424 for information about the Landry family reunion. If you have historical or genealogical information on the Ac
2 CONC adian Gravois, Mouton or LeBlanc families or other Acadian families, please contact the CONGRES MONDIAL ACADIEN LOUISIANE 1999 at C.P. 3804; Lafayette, LA 70502-3804. If you are interested in participating in the organization of a
2 CONC  family reunion for the 1999 CMA, please contact the same address or call (318)234-6166 or toll free from the US and Canada at (888)526-1999. This Acadian family history is sponsored by the CONGRES MONDIAL ACADIEN- LOUISIANE 1999 
2 CONC and its commercial partners, OUR LADY OF LOURDES REGIONAL MEDICAL CENTER, THE LOUISIANA LOTTERY, ACADIAN AMBULANCE AND AIRMED SERVICES, the LAFAYETTE CONSOLIDATED GOVERNMENT, the TERREBONNE PARISH GOVERNMENT, and THE FONDATION COD
2 CONC OFIL.
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from: <http://www.landrystuff.com/Landrys%20of%20Acadia.htm>
2 CONT
2 CONT LANDRYS  OF  ACADIA
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Professor White, there is no specific documentation to show that the ancestors of the Acadian Landrys actually originated from LaChaussee. Even though it is known that a majority of the first colonists in Acadia came 
2 CONC from Loundunais [Genevieve Massignon, in her linguistic analysis] and there were numerous Landrys in the vicinity of LaChaussee in the 17th century, there is no certainty that the Landrys to arrive in Acadia were from that area.
2 CONT
2 CONT The task of establishing the progenitor of the Landry's of Acadia is problematical to say the least. Researchers have established that there were two Rene Landrys to settle early in Acadia. Some investigators have named one Rene L
2 CONC andry, native of LaChausee, as the founder of the clan in Acadia. He shows to be born ca 1618. He arrived ca 1640 and there married Perrine Bourg. The other Rene Landry shows to be born ca 1634, probably in Martiaze or Loudon, Fra
2 CONC nce. His parents are unknown. It is believed he married a Marie Bernard in 1659 while still in France. He arrived in Acadia that same year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some researchers have attempted to link the two Renes as father and son. Some Acadian records list them as Rene L'aine [the elder] and Rene le Jeunne [the younger]. However, that is questionable due to several facts. Rene L'aine w
2 CONC as born in 1618 while Rene le Jeunne was born in 1634. If this is correct, there would only be 16 years difference between the two. However, Father Clarence d'Entremont, historian and genealogist from Novia Scotia, states
2 CONT
2 CONT "I have an example in Acadia of a child when he was born had a father 15 years of age".
2 CONT
2 CONT However, Rene L'aine arrived in Acadia ca 1640 whereas Rene le Jeune arrived in 1659, suggesting a seperation would have existed between the two. Father Archange Godbout [who was in his time one of the most outstanding genealogist
2 CONC s in French Canada] in his "Dictionanaire des Acadiens" say plainly on page 423 that the second Rene Landry, born 1634, "came from France with his wife". [Venu de France avec sa femme, marie vers 1659 a Marie Bernard].
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert C. West in his "Atlas of Louisiana Surnames of French and Spanish Origin" on p.9 and 12 states: Rene Landry, le jeunne, emigrated from France, with his wife Marie Bernard, to Acadie about 1659". Some researchers suggest tha
2 CONC t Rene le Jeunne married after arriving in Acadia. This is based on records "Rene De La Heve Landry (---de France) m. at Port Royal vers 1659 Marie Bernard (---de France)" found in the National Canadiens Francais , Vol. I-II, p.75
2 CONC 1.
2 CONT
2 CONT Victor Landry claims that Rene Landry le Jeunne emigrated to Acadia in 1653 with a group that included Emmanuel Leborgne and Marie Bernard, who latter became Rene's wife. [Victor Landry...MY FAMILY-THE LANDRY GENEALOGY (as given b
2 CONC y an older member of the family).] However, no documentation can be found to support this position.
2 CONT
2 CONT Furthurmore, some researchers have shown Rene le Jeune to be the son of a Jean-Claude Landry and Marie Sale'. Writings in the publication" Familles Acadienne", Father Leopold Lanctot makes suggestions to the beginnings of the Land
2 CONC rys in the New World. He states
2 CONT
2 CONT "It all began in the year 1640 when a group of 10 from the Landry family came to Acadia from France. The Landry family were originally from La Ventrouze, near Mortagne-au-Perche, Department of Orne, France. They were encouraged to
2 CONC  come to Acadia by Marguerite Landry, daughter of Jean-Claude, and her husband Robert Martin. They had been in Acadia for several years.
2 CONT
2 CONT The group of 10 consisted of Jean-Claude Landry, and three children from his first marriage; twin Rene (22) and Antoinette (22), and Perrine (29) with her first husband Jacques Joffriau. Also was Jean-Claude's wife by second marri
2 CONC age, Marie Sale'(40) and three children from her first marriage to Martin Aucoin. These children were Michelle (22), Francois (18), and Jeannne Aucoin (8). Also in the group was Rene (6), son of Jean-Claude and Marie. The group pr
2 CONC obably settled near the river Saint-John in the Cape Sable area. They later moved to Port Royal. Please note that there were two named Rene in this group; Rene (the elder) , son of Jean-Claude by his first marriage, and Rene (the 
2 CONC younger), son of Jean-Claude and Marie. Rene (the elder) married Perrine Bourg in 1645."
2 CONT
2 CONT This infomation was also found in the "Histoire et Geneologic des Acadians" by Arsenault on pp 623-624. "Jean-Claude Landry (born 1593) and Marie Sale' (born 1600), daughter of Jean Denys Sale' and Francoise Arnaud, were married i
2 CONC n Orne Department, France, in 1633. This was a second marriage for both. One child was born from this marriage, Rene, born in 1634. Jean-Claude died in 1671 in Mortagne-Au-Perche, France.
2 CONT
2 CONT The name of Marie Sale', age 86, appears in the 1686 Census of Acadia living with her son "Rene." This is somewhat based on the information obtained in the 1686 census where Marie Sale' (widow of Jean Claude) is shown residing nex
2 CONC t door to Rene Landry and beyond Rene's oldest son, Antoine. In discussing Marie Sale's relationship to Rene Landry, Father d'Entremont states the following:
2 CONT
2 CONT "The listing of Marie Sale', age 86, in close proximity to Rene Landry and his family in the 1686 census, for some time caused confusion, amongst early genealogists, concerning the parents of Rene "le Jeune" Landry." Father d'Entr
2 CONC emont continues "that Bona Arsenault, in his 1978 publication, p.624..footnote71, says that according to Father Godbout, Rene Landry [husband of Marie Bernard] would have had as a mother Marie Sale'.
2 CONT
2 CONT This prompted a series of errors, one of which was to create a fictious Jean-Claude Landry, because Marie Sale' is said to be the widow of Jean Claude, who is in fact believed to have been a Micmac Indian. NOWHERE in any census or
2 CONC  other documents is there to be found an Acadian by the name Jean-Claude Landry."
2 CONT
2 CONT In a recent letter dated July 30, 1997, to Dr. Don Landry, Father d'Entremont reinforces this by the following "Jehan (or John) Claude was an indian, of the Micmac tribe. His name occurs twice in the Port Royal Church registers, A
2 CONC LWAYS as Jehan Clause, NEVER given as family name Landry. As a matter of fact, if "Clause" had not been his family name, it would mean that a register gives him his first and SECOND name. Moreover, the registers of old ALWAYS give
2 CONC  the WHOLE name of persons; but the fact is that Jehan Clause has his name given thus, NEVER with another name added to those two. If the family name had been omitted in the registers, it would be the only time that such a thing o
2 CONC ccurs in any register. Thus CLAUDE was the family name.
2 CONT
2 CONT I do not have Father Leopold Lanctot's work "Familles Acadiennes". But I have of him L'ACADIE DES ORIGINES-1603-1771. I just took it out of my library and all along the margin of the eight first chapters of this work, I have writt
2 CONC en (in the past) all along the margins FALSE and ERROR, etc. While reading it, I must have put the book aside after the 10th chapter on the account of the amount of errors I found in that book. I must say that in the work, he give
2 CONC s again, Landry as the family name of Jehan Clause. I must add that up to about 50 years ago, there were indians living in this vicinity of the Pubnicos. Some of these families had as a family name "Glaude". I always asked myself 
2 CONC if this "Glaude" was not a corruption of "Clause" Father d'Entremont continues
2 CONT
2 CONT "So, who was the father and mother of Rene Landry? I do not know nor does anybody know....Thus the descendants of Rene Landry....in my humble opinion, cannot go furthur up in genealogy on the Landry side, as we do not know who the
2 CONC  parents of this Rene Landry were!"
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Bernard was born ca.1645 probably in Beauvoir-Sur-Mer, France. There is some question also as to who her parents were. There is considerable proof that her mother was "Marie" Andree Guyon (Guidon). Professor White in his "Di
2 CONC ctionaire genealogique des families acadiennes, partie I (1636-1714) " states ....."Marie Bernard d/o (?? Bernard) & Andree Guion , born 1645". However, Bona Arcenault's "Histoire et genealogie des Acadians", Vol 2, p428-429, stat
2 CONC es that Marie Bernard's father was Andre Bernard:
2 CONT
2 CONT ANDRE BERNARD, born 1620, Macon, originating in Beauvoir-sur-Mer, arrived in Acadia in 1641, "pour aller servir en l'habitation" for Charles de Latour, a Jemseg, on the river Saint-Jean in New Brunswick. He is the father of Jeanne
2 CONC , born 1643; Marie, born 1645; Nicholas, born 1662; and Rene, born 1663. "Apres la prise du fort de Jemseg par D'Aulnay", in 1645, he returned to France to return to Acadia "apres la rehabitation de Charles de LaTour, par la cour 
2 CONC de France." According to footnotes on page 428, Andre Bernard "Lors de la prise du fort de Jemseg, par Charles d' Aulnay, gouverneur d'Acadia, le 17 avril 1645, plusieurs compagnons de Charles de LaTour, faits prisonnies, ont ete 
2 CONC pendus sur place, apres deliberation du Conseil, pour servir d'exemple et de memoire a la poste-rite, d'une si obstinee rebellion. Andre Bernard est epargne et il en signe l'attestation, le 15 mai 1645. (Couillard, Charles de Sain
2 CONC t-Etienne de La Tour, p.489).
2 CONT
2 CONT Disputing the fact that Andre Bernard was Marie Bernard's father is the following from Bona Arcenaults publication quoting "Father Godbout indicates that the mother of the child Marie Bernard is Andree Guion. Her father is not And
2 CONC re Bernard......."
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite all the confusion to this point, it is agreed upon that Rene Landry le Jeunne and Marie Bernard were married and in Acadia in 1659. Together, they had fifteen (15) children, all born at Port Royal. They were Antoine, born 
2 CONC 1660; Claude, 1663; Cecile, 1664; Jean, 1666; Rene, 1668; Marie, 1670; Marguerite, 1672; Germain, 1674; Jeanne, 1676; Abraham, 1678; Pierre, 1680; Catherine, 1682; Anne, 1684; Charles, 1688; and Isabelle, 1691.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rene Landry died ca. 1692. In the 1714 census, it is indicated that Rene Landry died sometime before this census and his widow, Marie Bernard, was living in Port Royal with one of her sons. Marie Bernard died on January 11, 1719 a
2 CONC s document by Winston DeVille's "Acadian Church Records" vol 4 p.124-125. He also makes reference in his writings that "On April1, 1718, Marie Bernard, widow of Rene Landry, baptised a baby because of the danger in transporting th
2 CONC e infant in cold weather."
2 CONT
2 CONT The Landry clan grew over the years and spread in various parts of Acadia. Before the Great Derangement, the Landry family was established at Port Royal, Grand Pre', Beaubassin, Chipoudie, Petcoudiac, Port-Toulouse, Royal Island, 
2 CONC Port-Lajoye, and Isle St. John. Of particular  interest to the descendants of Rene Landry le Jeune, one Firmin Landry is of importance because he was the connecting factor between Acadia and Louisana. However, confusion and errors
2 CONC  also exists around this Firmin Landry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bona Arcenault has made claim that Firmin was "probably" the son of Pierre Landry and Marguerite Forest in his publication "Histoire et Genealogie des Acadians" vol 6, page 2522 "Firmin Landry, born 1728 probably the son of Pierre
2 CONC  and Marguerite Forest..." and in Vol 4 p.1406 "without doubt the son of Pierre and Marguerite....."
2 CONT
2 CONT (continued on following internal textfile)
2 CONT (continued from previous internal textfile)
2 CONT
2 CONT M.E."Mitch" Conover believes that Firmin was the son of Charles Landry and Marie Leblanc. He bases his believe on succession records in the St. Martinville Book of Original Acts whereby certain birth, baptismal and marriage certif
2 CONC icates appear. John T. Harrell also believes that Firmin was the son of Charles and Marie. He bases his assumption on the close relationship of Marie LeBlance, widow of Charles Landry listed in the 1763 census of Oxford Maryland, 
2 CONC placing Firmin Landry and Amant Landry among the known children of Charles Landry and Marie Leblanc. Firmin is later listed in the Attakapas census of 1777 as living next door to Amant Landry. Amant is later proven to be the son o
2 CONC f Charles and Marie.
2 CONT
2 CONT Many researchers believe that he was the son of Alexandre Landry and Ann Flan. This believe is based on Father Hebert's listings in his southwest Louisiana Records as follows: "Landry Firmin (Alexandre & ------, of Acadie) m. Theo
2 CONC tiste Thiboudau....." (SM ch: v4, #231)(Hebert vol 1)
2 CONT
2 CONT In a letter written to Dr. Donald Landry, Metairie, Louisiana, in response to a letter written about questions concerning Firmin Landry, Professor Stephen A. White states: "Regarding Firmin Landry, I am aware of the hypothesis tha
2 CONC t has recently been advanced to the effect that Firmin who married Francois Thibodeau and Theotiste Thibodeau was a son of Charles Landry and Marie Leblanc. The researchers who have reached this conclusion seem to have some reason
2 CONC  for rejecting what appears to be recorded in Firmin's burial record at St.Martinville [SM ch: v4 #231] [4Feb. 1801 ag age 67 yrs] which according to Father Donald Hebert [Southwest Louisiana Records, vol 1, p.344] shows that Theo
2 CONC tiste Thibodeau's husband was the son of Alexandre Landry. I do not know what their reason might be for this, and consequently I am not changed in my belief that Firmin Landry was the son of Alexandre Landry and Marguerite Blancha
2 CONC rd. Insofar as I know, Marguerite Blanchard's husband was the only Alexandre Landry who was fathering children in Acadia around the time of Firmin Landry's birth. Please note that Alexandre Landry who married Anne Flan could not h
2 CONC ave been Firmin's father because he and Anne were only married in 1732."
2 CONT
2 CONT To furthur support Professor White's opinion, the following is loosely translated from a Spanish document found in St. Martinville "1801-entry 231-Firmin Landry--Day four of February of the year eighteen hundred and one, I Don Mig
2 CONC uel Berndo Barriere priest of this church of St. Martin of the juristiction of Atakapas, buried in the 5th camp of the referred church, the body of Firmin Landry adult of age sixty and days and six years, legitimate son of Alexand
2 CONC re Landry and of ----------, natural of the Acadia, which was married with Theotiste Thibodau, did not receive the sacrements (the last rites), for having died suddenly, did not give testament an ( ): For the record was signed by 
2 CONC Migel Bernado Barriere, curator.
2 CONT
2 CONT Based upon this information, the assumption must be made that Firmin was indeed the son of an Alexandre Landry. The question now is who are the parents of this Alexandre? Professor White states that Alexandre was the son of Germai
2 CONC n Landry in this letter to B.R.Malkmus of Decatur, Alabama "Once we eliminate from consideration the Alexandre Landry who married Anne Flan, the only other Alexandre known who might have been Firmin's father was Germain's son. As 
2 CONC it happens that Alexandre Landry's other children, including Genevieve, were all in Oxford, Maryland, in close proximity to Firmin. There is certainly a certain amount of plausability to the identification I have proposed."
2 CONT
2 CONT Germain Landry was the son of Rene Landry and Marie Bernard. He was born in 1674 at Port Royal, Acadia. He married in 1694 Marie Melanson, the daughter of Pierre Melanson and Marie Mius d'Entremont.. Marie was born in 1673 at Gran
2 CONC d Pre'. The family was established at St. Famillie Parish, Pisiguit. The census of 1717 shows them having 6 boys and 6 girls; Alexandre, Abraham, Germain, Paul, Pierre, Marie, Marguerite, a missing boy and a missing girl. Germain 
2 CONC died after the census of 1714.
2 CONT
2 CONT Now that some sort of ancestory has been established from Rene Landry to Firmin Landry, it is possible to move on with Firmin's history. He was born ca. 1728 in Pisiguit, married Francoise "Elizabeth" Thibodeau in 1752 at Pisiguit
2 CONC . They had issue of four children: Joseph, born 1753; Saturin, born 1755; Helene, born 1757; and Marie Madeleine, born 1759. He is remarried by 1770 to Theotiste "Sally" Thibodeaux, daughter of Charles Thibodeaux and Brigette Brea
2 CONC ux. They have issue of ten children: Francoise (in memory of his first wife), born 1770; Hubert, 1773; Helene, 1774; Rosalie, 1776; Valentin, 1778; Marie, 1780; Alexandre, 1782; Agnes, 1784; Marguerite, 1789; and Madeleine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Firmin was deported to Maryland in 1755 with his first wife Francoise Thibodeau and three children. Proof of that is found in the FRENCH PRESENCE IN MARYLAND, p.193 "Acadians in the 1763 census--Oxford". "Landry Firmin, Elizabeth 
2 CONC his wife, Joseph and Saturin, Eleine and Magdeleine". In this record Francoise is called Elizabeth, but this is the only mention of her under that given name, and may simply be an error.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Expulsion began sometime in November, 1755, when the village of Grand Pre, consisting to two parishes St. Charles at Riviere-Aux-Canards and St. Joseph at Grand Pre and little houses spread out for a mile and a half was burned
2 CONC  to the ground. Of the 1600 people captured in that region, 178 were placed aboard the vessel Elizabeth and 242 aboard the Leopard, and were shipped to Maryland. These two vessels were joined by the Dolphin with 227 aboard and the
2 CONC  Ranger with 323 aboard.
2 CONT
2 CONT Not knowing what to do with them, a decision was made by the authorities, to send two of the ships to the Eastern shore and two were kept on the western side on Chesapeke Bay. After this, the Acadians were to be scattered througho
2 CONC ut the colony, some to Oxford, which included Firmin and his family. A Maryland paper wrote at the time "Refugees, especially French Catholics without funds, found chilly reception here" and "Gruging charity cares for Acadian 'pes
2 CONC ts'. Because of the fierce winter storms, the Dolphin and the Ranger had to sail into the Boston Harbor and seek shelter for a number of days and finally reached Maryland on November 30, 1755, just as waives of Francophobia (Engli
2 CONC sh dislike for the French) and anti-Catholicism in the colonies had crested. Because of the dreadful overcrowding and the delay due to the storms, the ships supplies were depleted. The captain of the ships on December 8, 1755, unc
2 CONC eremoniously dumped 181 Acadians, including Firmin and his family, on the warf, gruffly telling them he had no more provisions to give them, and as far as he was concerned, they could starve. He then sailed away leaving them among
2 CONC st a population who hated everything they stood for. Rumors spread throughout the English colony of Oxford that the Catholic Acadians were plotting to stir up insurrection amongst the negro slaves, capture the colony and turn it o
2 CONC ver to the Catholic France. In the midst of all this, stood the Acadians. They were there through no fault of their own. Their only "crime" had been the refusal to take the oath of allegience to England.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Acadians were quartered virually as prisoners, not to leave town without a written permit of the selectmen under penalty of five days in prison or ten lashes, they depended on the cold hand of the public for food, clothes, and
2 CONC  lodging and were given some provisions. Governors of the colonies received the Acadians with varying degrees of hostility. While at Oxford, the Acadians petitioned the Maryland legislature for relief and on August 1756, at the Ta
2 CONC lbot County Court, a petition was presented "setting forth that Joseph Bujiale, Fermee and Charles Landree, French neutrals, have each three small children, the oldest of which is not over five years of age...that they cannot supp
2 CONC ort their families, and can hardly get bread for themselves". The justices of the court allowed each 500 pounds of tobacco, then used as money in Maryland.
2 CONT
2 CONT While in Maryland, the Acadians fell victim of malnutrition and disease. Between 1755 and 1763, the Acadian population in Maryland declined from 913 to 667! By the mid 1760's, the Acadians were anxious to leave Maryland and the na
2 CONC tive Marylanders were equally anxious to see them leave. In the late spring of 1765, one Landry (probably Joseph) addressed a petition to Governor Sharpe, on behalf of the Acadians in Maryland and Pennsylvania to be allowed to set
2 CONC tle in Novia Scotia, either on the Bay of Gaspe or Chaleur. Frustrated in their attempts to resettle in Acadia, they requested settlement in Louisiana. Therefore, joined by the surviving Acadian relatives and friends in Pennsylvan
2 CONC ia, they pooled their resources, chartered ships, and made preparations to sail to Louisiana. However, it wasn't until March 1767 that departure actually took place.
2 CONT
2 CONT The exact date that Firmin  left Maryland for Louisiana is unknown. Records show him still in Maryland on January 14, 1766, when he witnessed a wedding.  However, Firmin's first wife, Francoise, must have died prior to his leaving
2 CONC  Maryland. Theotiste "Sally' Thibadeau, Firmin's second wife,  must have preceeded Firmin to Louisiana  because she arrived before the1766 census of the Acadian Coast in Louisiana where she is listed  as the widow Godin along with
2 CONC  her 5 year old daughter Barbe. She had been married ca. 1757 to Bonaventure Godin. The date of Firmin's marriage to Thotiste is uncertain but it was prior to 1770 because he had a daughter named Francoise, "in memory of his first
2 CONC  wife", born October 22, 1770 and  the mother is listed as Theotiste Thibodeau (SMCH: V1 p.20). Giving the announcement of Banns at the church, the time of search to make sure of their kindred, plus a nine month pregnancy, would i
2 CONC ndicate that they were married probably  in mid-to-late 1769. The earliest listing found of Firmin Landry in Louisiana is in the Brand Book at St. Martinville dated 1769, "Records of Attakapas District, Louisiana 1739-1811". Bona 
2 CONC Arcenault shows on page 1063 of "Attakapas Et Opelousas" Firman appearing in the 1769 Opelousas Brand Book. Assuming that Firmin had to have been in Louisiana early enough to have developed a need to register his brand, one could 
2 CONC say he arrived as early as late 1766 or 1767. On June 20, 1771, he is shown to receive a land grant in the Attakapas District [Land Records of the Attakapas District by Glen R. Conrad] which would have been on the upper Bayou Verm
2 CONC illion, near Grand Pointe. Later he obtained a smaller tract on the Teche at Fausse Pointe near present Loreauville.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are lists that show when many Acadians from Maryland arrived in New Orleans, but Firmin Landry is not listed on any of them. "I beleive that Firmin Landry must have brought his family to Louisiana by ship, as there was no ot
2 CONC her practical method of making the trip at that time", Professor Stephan A. White.
2 CONT
2 CONT Firmin's son Saturin apparently remained single, but his other son Joseph settled on Bayou Vermillion and became the progenitor of what is today's Landry clan in that area.
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------
