1 NAME George /Gordon/
2 GIVN George
2 _MARNM Gordon
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1615
2 ADDR
3 CITY Huntly
3 CTRY Scotland
1 BURI
2 DATE 2 JUL 1645
2 ADDR
3 CITY BURIAL Old Machar Churchyard Old Aberdeen, Aberdeen City, Scotland  MEMORIAL ID	157731091
1 DEAT
2 DATE 2 JUL 1645
2 ADDR
3 CITY Alford
3 CTRY Scotland
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Eldest son of George Gordon, eldest son of George Gordon, Earl of Enzie and later second marquess of Huntly, and his wife, Lady Anne Campbell, daughter of Archibald Campbell, 7th earl of Argyll.  Killed at the Batt
2 CONC le of Alford. He died unmarried and left no children, and was subsequently buried with great ceremony in St Machar's Cathedral. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Battle of Alford, 1645 Although he had defeated one Covenanter army at Auldearn, the Marquis of Montrose had to overcome Lieutenant-General Baillie's main force before he could move out of the Highlands and into central Scotla
2 CONC nd. Alasdair MacColla returned to western Scotland to raise reinforcements while Montrose and Baillie once again spent several weeks manoeuvring across Moray and Aberdeenshire trying to gain a tactical advantage.
2 CONT
2 CONT The two armies finally met early in July 1645 at Alford, twenty miles west of Aberdeen. While Baillie approached from the north, Montrose took up a strong position on Gallows Hill overlooking a ford across the swift-flowing River 
2 CONC Don. He placed most of his troops on the reverse slope of the hill out of sight with a small force on the crest in order to encourage Baillie to advance. As Montrose expected, Baillie thought that the Royalist troops were retreati
2 CONC ng and sent his cavalry across the ford to outflank them. As soon as most of the Covenanter horse had crossed onto the marshy ground on the south side of the river, Montrose ordered his whole army to advance to the crest of the hi
2 CONC ll. Unable to retreat safely, Baillie was forced to deploy close to the Don in an area of marshy ground, using hedgerows and wet ditches to strengthen his position. Both sides drew up in standard formation, with two cavalry wings 
2 CONC and infantry in the centre.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Covenanter army comprised six regular infantry regiments (Cassillis's, Elcho's, Lanark's, Moray's, Glencairn's, Callendar's) and a seventh regiment of Aberdeenshire levies, a total of around 2,400 men. The 600 Covenanter horse
2 CONC  were commanded by Lord Balcarres on the left flank and Sir James Halkett on the right. Montrose had about the same number of foot but only 300 horse. Lord Gordon's cavalry were placed on the Royalist right wing and Lord Aboyne's 
2 CONC on the left, with Irish companies in support. In the centre were the Strathbogie regiment, Colonel Farquarson of Inverary's regiment, the MacDonald Highlanders and Manus O'Cahan's Irish companies. A small reserve under the command
2 CONC  of Lord Napier was placed in the rear.
2 CONT
2 CONT Baillie was reluctant to advance against the strong Royalist position on Gallows Hill. The battle began when Lord Gordon charged down the hill against Lord Balcarres' cavalry, who were veterans of Marston Moor. In the first signif
2 CONC icant cavalry fight of the civil wars in Scotland, Balcarres held his ground against the Royalists and almost succeeded in driving them back until Lieutenant-Colonel Laghtnan led the supporting Irishmen in a decisive intervention.
2 CONC  Throwing down their pikes and muskets, they got in among the Covenanter formation and used swords and dirks to hamstring their horses. Panic quickly set in, and Balcarres' surviving troopers broke and fled.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the opposite wing, the cavalry of Lord Aboyne and Sir James Halkett were engaged in an ineffectual exchange of carbine and pistol fire. Lord Gordon led his cavalry right around the rear of the Covenanter army to fall upon Halke
2 CONC tt's troopers from behind. The Covenanter horse were quickly routed, but Lord Gordon was killed during the confused fighting. According to tradition, he was shot in the back by a stray bullet fired by one of his own men.
2 CONT
2 CONT The routing of the Covenanter horse on both flanks soon led to the collapse of the infantry in the centre. Having stood firm in a firefight with the Royalist foot, the Covenanters were overwhelmed and broken when attacked from the
2 CONC  flank and rear by the Royalist horse. The fleeing Covenanters were cut down as they tried to escape back across the ford.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lieutenant-General Baillie offered his resignation after the defeat at Alford and Parliament decided to recall Major-General Monro from Ulster to command all forces in Scotland. However, Baillie was obliged to continue his command
2 CONC  until Monro's arrival. Meanwhile, a deputation from the Committee of Estates, including the Marquis of Argyll, Lord Balfour of Burleigh and several leading clergymen, accompanied Baillie on campaign to offer him advice on strateg
2 CONC y and tactics.
2 CONT
2 CONT The battle of Kilsyth » Sources: S.R. Gardiner, History of the Great Civil War vol. ii (London 1889)
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Gaunt, The Cromwellian Gazetteer (Stroud 1987)
2 CONT
2 CONT Stuart Reid, Auldearn 1645: the Marquis of Montrose's Scottish campaign (Osprey 2003)
2 CONT
2 CONT William Seymour, Battles in Britain 1066-1746 (Ware 1997)
2 CONT
2 CONT David Stevenson, Revolution & Counter-Revolution in Scotland 1644-51 (Newton Abbott 1977)
2 CONT
2 CONT Trevor Royle: Civil War: the wars of the Three Kingdoms 1638-60 (London 2004)
2 CONT
2 CONT Links: Alford UK Battlefields Resource Centre
