2 NOTE The Duke of Rothes died at Holyrood Palace in Edinburgh, 1681. His body was transported back to Leslie House, Fife, for internment in the family vault. The order of precedence in the procession for the many civil and military bodi
3 CONC es wishing to be represented was laid down by The Lord Lyon and published in four sheets. The procession was said to be 17 miles long.
3 CONT Charles ll offered to pay for the funeral expenses but regrettably by the time the total had been worked out he was no longer on the throne and his son, James, refused to have anything to do with it. It was said by historians to h
3 CONC ave been 'a great financial burden upon the family for many years...'  Credit: Clan Leslie Trust
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/2f/d3/a3/42/5344483a51b0ca8a/john_duke_of_rothes_b_original.jpg?hash=a738d07867c7ed836f8feec2e73467c6a8105a2a667a1143ce4b35659eb37a20.1797148799
2 FORM jpg
2 NOTE Inscribed 'John, Duke of Rothes'.
3 CONT In 1651 he carried the Sword of State at the Coronation of Charles ll at Scone. Captured at the battle of Worcester 1651, returned to Scotland 1655 and finally liberated 1658 on payment of a fine to Oliver Cromwell. After Charles 
3 CONC ll Restoration 1660, appointed President of the Privy Council of Scotland and 1661 Lord High Chancellor for life. 1680 He was created Duke of Rothes, Marquis of Ballinbreich, Earl of Leslie, Viscount Lugtoun, Lord Auchmoutie and C
3 CONC askieberrie. (See Charter B4). These titles were conferred on him and his heirs male only so the Dukedom died with him in 1681. A previous charter obtained in 1663 enabled the Earldom to pass through the female line so on his deat
3 CONC h his elder daughter, Margaret, succeeded as a Countess in her own right.
3 CONT He died at Holyrood in Edinburgh 27 July 1681. (M7,M11,M12) As a great statesman he was awarded a state funeral. The procession bearing the body back to Leslie for burial was said to be 17 miles long.(E63)
1 OBJE
