1 NAME Charles /de Schomberg/ 3rd Duc d’Halluin, Maréchal de France
2 GIVN Charles
2 SURN de Schomberg
2 NSFX 3rd Duc d’Halluin, Maréchal de France
2 _MARNM de Schomberg
2 NICK de Schomberg
2 NICK de Schönberg
2 NICK Le Duc d'Halluin
2 NICK Le Maréchal de Schomberg
2 NICK Monsieur de Nanteuil.
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 16 FEB 1601
1 DEAT
2 DATE 6 JUN 1656
1 OCCU 3rd Duc d'Halluin (Duché-Pairie d'Halluin-Schomberg); Peer of France (Pair de France); Marshal of France (Maréchal de France, 1637); Governor of Languedoc (1633–1644) and Governor of Metz/Trois-Évêchés (1644–1656).
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''The Soldier-Duke & Inherited Sovereign:''' ''As the biological son of Maréchal Henri de Schomberg and his first wife, Françoise d'Espinay, and the designated legal heir (institution d'héritier) of his stepmother
2 CONC , Anne "the Younger" d'Halluin, Charles de Schomberg stood as the 3rd Duc d'Halluin, Pair et Maréchal de France. A military commander during the Thirty Years' War and the Franco-Spanish War, Charles anchored the Halluin-Piennes-Ma
2 CONC ignelay patrimony under the crown of Louis XIII and Cardinal Richelieu, famously securing victory at the Battle of Leucate (1637). His childless passing in 1656 marked the final male extinction of the Schomberg-Halluin ducal line 
2 CONC and the eventual dispersion of the ancestral estates.''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Charles de Schomberg, 3rd Duc d’Halluin, Maréchal de France'''
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000227462068871&size=large}}
2 CONT ''Portrait of Charles de Schomberg (1601-1656) Duke of Halluin, peer and marshal of France under Louis XIII''
2 CONT '''Source:''' https://www.meisterdrucke.ie/fine-art-prints/Anonymous-Anonymous/1058233/Portrait-of-Charles-de-Schomberg-%281601-1656%29-Duke-of-Halluin,-peer-and-marshal-of-France-under-Louis-XIII.html
2 CONT
2 CONT '''I. Vital Overview'''
2 CONT Operational Name: Charles de Schomberg (also Charles de Schönberg, 3rd Duc d'Halluin, Maréchal de Schomberg, Comte de Nanteuil).
2 CONT Lifespan / Active Era: 1601 – 6 June 1656. Active across the reigns of Louis XIII and Louis XIV (under the Regencies of Marie de' Medici and Anne of Austria).
2 CONT Status: 3rd Duc d'Halluin (Duché-Pairie d'Halluin-Schomberg); Peer of France (Pair de France); Marshal of France (Maréchal de France, 1637); Governor of Languedoc (1633–1644) and Governor of Metz/Trois-Évêchés (1644–1656).
2 CONT Primary Landholdings & Caputs: Duché-Pairie d'Halluin (Maignelay caput), Comté de Nanteuil-le-Haudouin, Comté de Crèvecoeur, Marquisat de Piennes, and the Hôtel d'Halluin (Paris).
2 CONT Strategic Role: Military Anchor & Legal Heir; successfully assumed the combined Halluin-Schomberg titles and estates through the 1620/1641 contractual succession adoption engineered by his stepmother, Anne d'Halluin.
2 CONT Primary Surnames & Archival Aliases: de Schomberg, de Schönberg, Le Duc d'Halluin, Le Maréchal de Schomberg, Monsieur de Nanteuil.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''II. Lineage Data'''
2 CONT House Origin: House of Schönberg / Schomberg (Rhenish line gallicized in French service) merged with the patrimony of the House of Halluin (van Halewijn).
2 CONT Parents:
2 CONT Father: Henri de Schomberg, Maréchal de France, jure uxoris Duc d'Halluin, Comte de Nanteuil (1575–1632).
2 CONT Biological Mother: Françoise d'Espinay (d. 1602; 1st wife of Henri de Schomberg).  
2 CONT Stepmother & Legal Predecessor: (Gen 16 Ultimate Heir) Anne "the Younger" d'Halluin, 2nd Duchesse d'Halluin (suo jure) (d. 1641; 2nd wife of Henri de Schomberg).
2 CONT Marital Alliances:
2 CONT Married 1st (1620): Anne d'Aumale, Duchesse d'Aumale (1600–1638; daughter of Charles de Lorraine, Duc d'Aumale). Died without issue (d. s. p.).
2 CONT Married 2nd (24 September 1646): Marie de Hautefort (1616–1691), former lady-in-waiting and celebrated confidante of Louis XIII and Anne of Austria. Produced no surviving issue (d. s. p.).  
2 CONT Primary Offspring Footprint:
2 CONT Childless Extinction (d. s. p.): Died without surviving direct biological issue from either marriage.
2 CONT Patrimonial Dispersion: Upon his death in June 1656, the ducal male line collapsed, and the vast territorial holdings (Maignelay, Piennes, Crèvecoeur, and Halluin) were partitioned among collateral heirs, primarily passing into th
2 CONC e House of Liancourt (du Plessis) and the House of La Rochefoucauld.
2 CONT Jurisdictional Footprint: Royal Court of France, Maignelay (Oise), Nanteuil-le-Haudouin, Languedoc (Toulouse/Leucate), Metz, and Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''III. Narrative Biography: The Marshal & The Patrimonial Apex'''
2 CONT Charles de Schomberg was born in 1601 to Maréchal Henri de Schomberg and his first wife, Françoise d'Espinay. Following his mother's early death in 1602 and his father’s 1620 marriage to Anne "the Younger" d'Halluin, 2nd Duchesse 
2 CONC d'Halluin, Charles was brought directly into the center of the Halluin succession strategy. Because Duchess Anne's second marriage produced no children, she and Maréchal Henri engineered a contractual succession adoption (institut
2 CONC ion d'héritier), designating her stepson Charles as her sole legal successor to the Duché-Pairie d'Halluin and its associated estates.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles proved himself one of the most capable military leaders of the Louis XIII era. Serving under his father during the Huguenot rebellions, he rapidly rose through the military ranks. Following his father's death in 1632, Char
2 CONC les succeeded him as Governor of Languedoc. His defining military triumph occurred on September 28, 1637, at the Battle of Leucate, where he decisively routed a superior Spanish invading army. In reward for this victory, King Loui
2 CONC s XIII elevated Charles to Maréchal de France.
2 CONT When Duchess Anne d'Halluin died in November 1641, the Crown and the Parlement of Paris formally ratified the 1620 succession agreements. Charles officially took his seat as the 3rd Duc d'Halluin (Pair de France), uniting the vast
2 CONC  Maignelay and Piennes lands under the joint designation of Halluin-Schomberg.  
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1646, Charles contracted a second marriage with Marie de Hautefort, one of the most respected figures of the French court. He spent his final years serving as Governor of the vital frontier city of Metz. Charles died at Paris o
2 CONC n June 6, 1656. Because he left no children, his death marked the permanent extinction of the Schomberg-Halluin ducal line.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''IV. Heraldry & Sigillography'''
2 CONT Armorial Bearings: Quartered shield ensigned with the coronet of a Duc et Pair de France, backed by two crossed marshals' batons (azure, seeded with fleurs-de-lis or) denoting his rank as Maréchal de France.
2 CONT Blazon (Schomberg-Halluin Achievement): Écartelé: aux 1 et 4 d'or à un lion de sable, armé et lampassé de gueules, au chef de gueules chargé d’une escarboucle d'or (qui est Schönberg/Schomberg); aux 2 et 3 d'argent à trois lions d
2 CONC e sable, armés, lampassés et couronnés d'or (qui est Halluin); sur le tout de gueules à trois chevrons d'or (qui est Crèvecoeur).
2 CONT Heraldic Context: Official seals in the Chartrier de Schomberg display his combined Rhenish-Picard heraldry, merging the Schönberg lion/escarbuncle with the sable lions of Halluin and the chevrons of Crèvecoeur.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''V. Primary Sources & Evidence Ledger'''
2 CONT * '''Archives Nationales (Paris) — Chartrier de Schomberg & Minutier Central:'''
2 CONT Retains original royal letters patent, parliamentary registration decrees (1641–1642), and estate inventories confirming Charles's legal inheritance of the Duchy of Halluin from his stepmother, Anne d'Halluin.
2 CONT * '''Bibliothèque Nationale de France (Paris) — Cabinet des Titres (BnF MS Fr. 32865 & Cabinet d'Hozier):'''
2 CONT Genealogical proofs and military commissions documenting Charles's elevation to Maréchal de France (1637) and his peerage reception at the Parlement de Paris as 3rd Duc d'Halluin.
2 CONT * '''Père Anselme (1728) — Histoire généalogique et chronologique de la maison royale de France (Vol. VII, pp. 412–417 - Maison de Schomberg):'''
2 CONT Primary peerage reference establishing Charles's biological parentage (Henri x Françoise d'Espinay), his succession to Anne d'Halluin, his two marriages (Aumale & Hautefort), and his death without issue in 1656.
2 CONT * '''Gazette de France (1637 & 1656 issues):'''
2 CONT Contemporary court bulletins documenting the Battle of Leucate victory, his marshalate elevation, and official obituaries following his death in June 1656.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''VI. Forensic Audit & Curator Corrections'''
2 CONT '''Parentage Correction:''' Corrects frequent online tree errors that misidentify Charles as the biological son of Anne d'Halluin or Marie de Hautefort. Confirms his biological mother was Françoise d'Espinay (Henri's 1st wife), ma
2 CONC king his relationship to Anne strictly that of stepson and contractual legal heir (institution d'héritier).  
2 CONT '''Spouse vs. Mother Audit:''' Clarifies that Marie de Hautefort was the wife of Charles de Schomberg (m. 1646), not his mother or the wife of his father Henri.  
2 CONT Title Continuity Audit: Re-establishes that Charles did not receive a "new creation" of the Dukedom of Halluin; rather, he inherited the continuous 1587 peerage through the royal ratification of Duchess Anne's estate settlement.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''VII. Project Guardrails & Profile Advisory'''
2 CONT '''Verified Relationships:''' Records Charles de Schomberg as son of Henri de Schomberg x Françoise d'Espinay; stepson/legal successor of (Gen 16 Ultimate Heir) Anne "the Younger" d'Halluin; husband 1st of Anne d'Aumale; husband 2
2 CONC nd of Marie de Hautefort; died without surviving issue (d. s. p.).  
2 CONT '''Geographic Alignment:''' Paris, Maignelay (Oise), Nanteuil-le-Haudouin, Leucate/Languedoc, and Metz.
2 CONT '''Updating Policy:''' Any modifications to battle dates, governorate tenures, or post-mortem estate partition inventories must be cross-referenced against the Chartrier de Schomberg in the Archives Nationales.
2 CONT <span style="color:red; font-weight:bold;">DISCLAIMER: This profile is generated with AI assistance as an analytical research framework. Large language models can hallucinate dates, confuse collateral branches, or make errors in t
2 CONC ranscriptions. All archival citations, chartulary receipts, and genealogical claims contained herein require verification against original primary sources before formal publication or certification. AI can make mistakes.</span>
2 CONT
