1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anne_d%27Arpajon
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne d'Arpajon (Anne Claude Louise d'Arpajon; 4 March 1729 - 27 June 1794) was a French aristocrat and first lady of honour to Queens of France, Marie Leszczyńska and Marie Antoinette. She was called "Madame Etiquette" by Marie An
2 CONC toinette for her insistence that no minutia of court etiquette ever be altered or disregarded.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and background
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father, Louis de Sévérac, Marquis of Arpajon-sur-Cère (1667–1736), bought the Marquisat of Saint-Germain-lès-Châtres in 1720, and was granted permission by Philippe d'Orléans (régent for Louis XV), to rename it Saint-Germain-l
2 CONC ès-Arpajon, and its seat Arpajon. Her mother, Anne Charlotte Le Bas de Montargis, was lady in waiting to the Duchess of Berry, daughter of the regent. Anne Claude married Philippe de Noailles, Duke of Mouchy, Captain of the Hunts 
2 CONC at Versailles, on 27 November 1741. Noailles was one of the leading families of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Relations with Marie Antoinette
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1770, she was made lady in waiting to the new Dauphine Marie Antoinette, upon her arrival in France. She met Marie Antoinette at the border, where she was a part of the French entourage, and was made responsible for her court a
2 CONC nd behaviour at Versailles. Marie Antoinette greatly disliked her, as she prevented her from doing things she liked with reference to court etiquette, and she gave her the name Madame Etiquette. When in 1774 Marie Antoinette becam
2 CONC e a queen, she fired the Countess of Noailles, prompting her to become a part of the noble opposition to the queen with the Kings' aunts, Mesdames, at Bellevue 
2 CONT
2 CONT The French Revolution
2 CONT
2 CONT Countess Anne and her husband Philippe were guillotined during the French Revolution on 27 June 1794. Many of her relatives met the same fate. On July 22, 1794, the widow, daughter-in-law, and granddaughter of Philippe's brother L
2 CONC ouis, 4th duc de Noailles, were guillotined. Louis's other granddaughter, Adrienne, wife of the Marquis de Lafayette, was saved by the intervention of America's Minister to France, James Monroe. They and the other nobles who died 
2 CONC at the guillotine are buried at Picpus Cemetery, which is also the final resting place of the Marquis and Marquise de Lafayette.
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.Louise Henriette Charlotte Philippine de Noailles (1745–1832).
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.Charles Adrien de Noailles (1747) Prince of Poix.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.Louis Philippe de Noailles (1748–1750) Prince of Poix.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.Daniel François Marie de Noailles (1750–1752) Marquis of Noailles, later Prince of Poix.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.Philippe de Noailles, Duke of Monchy (1752–1819).
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.Louis Marie de Noailles, Viscount of Noailles (1756–1804).
2 CONT
2 CONT In popular culture
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne d'Arpajon was played by Judy Davis in Marie Antoinette in 2006, and by Cora Witherspoon in Marie Antoinette in 1938.
