1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Julie d'Antin, last abbess of the Royal Abbey of Fontevraud'''
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000217256206847&size=large}}
2 CONT [https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Julie-Gilette_de_Pardaillan_d%27Antin,_last_abbess_of_Fontevraud_Abbey.jpg ''Julie-Gilette de Pardaillan d'Antin, last abbess of Fontevraud Abbey'']
2 CONT
2 CONT Julie Sophie Charlotte de Pardaillan d'Antin, the last abbess of Fontevraud Abbey, was born in 1725 and died in 1797. She served as abbess from 1765 to 1792. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Birth'''
2 CONT Julie-Sophie-Gillette de Pardaillan de Gondrin de Montespan d'Antin was born  into a prestigious aristocratic family in 1725 to Louis de Pardaillan de Gondrin (1707-1743) and Françoise Gillonne de Montmorency (1704-1768). 
2 CONT She  was the great-great-granddaughter of Madame de Montespan and the great-great-niece of the queen of abbesses Marie-Madeleine de Rochechouart. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Education'''
2 CONT Her education began at the age of three in She became the 36th and last abbess of the Royal Abbey of Fontevraud in 1765, the village, like the Abbey , where she had always resided, with the exception of three years spent at Court.
2 CONT
2 CONT During her stay at Fontevraud, she held various important positions, such as novice mistress, teacher to a royal princess and grand prioress, working closely with Abbess Marie-Louise de Valence.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Career as Abbess:'''
2 CONT Appointed as the 36th abbess in 1765 at the age of 40, she revived the public and social celebrations initiated by Gabrielle de Rochechouart. Since the order was answerable only to the Pope and the king, the abbess enjoyed a degre
2 CONC e of freedom that allowed her to increase the abbey's influence and make its assets prosper. Julie d'Antin was thus at the head of a powerful institution that brought together thousands of people.  and held that position until the
2 CONC  French Revolution, which led to the closure of the abbey in 1792. 
2 CONT During her tenure she was kept busy defending the rights and property of the convent, and, as the abbess of Fontevraud, was confronted with the protests of the monks who were becoming less and less tolerant of their condition.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Revolution'''
2 CONT Everything accelerated with the Revolution  : the Church's property was inventoried by the State in 1791, then monastic vows were banned, and finally the orders were abolished . In 1792, the nuns were expelled from the abbey befor
2 CONC e it was looted and transformed into a prison a few years later.
2 CONT
2 CONT She fled the abbey in 1792, disguised as a peasant, and died in poverty in Paris in 1797, five years after leaving Fontevraud. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Resources:'''
2 CONT *https://www.fontevraud.fr/julie-dantin-derniere-abbesse-de-labbaye-royale-de-fontevraud/
