1 NAME François Pons /de Salignac/ comte de la Mothe Fénelon
2 GIVN François Pons
2 SURN de Salignac
2 NSFX comte de la Mothe Fénelon
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Couvent des Minimes
2 ADDR
3 CITY Aubeterre-sur-Dronne
3 STAE Nouvelle-Aquitaine
3 POST 16390
3 CTRY France
1 BIRT
2 DATE 19 JAN 1630
2 PLAC Château de Fénélon, Périgord, France
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1679
1 OCCU priest, missionary
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT SALIGNAC DE LA MOTHE-FÉNELON, FRANÇOIS DE, priest, Sulpician, missionary; b. 1641 at the Château de Fénelon in Périgord (France); d. 1679 in France.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a half-brother of the famous archbishop of Cambrai, who was ten years his junior. Nothing is known of his early years, of his education, or of his studies. We do know however that in 1666 he was so eager to devote himself t
2 CONC o the missions in New France that he obtained permission to leave after spending only 15 months in the seminary in Paris. He set sail 30 Jan. 1667 and arrived at Quebec on 27 June. Bishop Laval* ordained him priest 11 June 1668.
2 CONT
2 CONT At this period those Iroquois who had settled down in the Kenté (Quinte) peninsula on Lake Ontario, came to Montreal to ask the superior of the seminary for missionaries. M. de Fénelon and M. Trouvé* made known their desire to acc
2 CONC ept the invitation. The superior, M. de Queylus [see Thubières] agreed to this and sent them both to Quebec to acquire the necessary civil and religious authorizations. They obtained from Governor Rémy de Courcelle a grant of land
2 CONC  on which to establish their mission and from Bishop Laval they received a warm letter of recommendation. On 28 Oct. 1668 they reached the village of Kenté. They spent the winter there.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Spring M. de Fénelon went down to Montreal and Quebec by canoe “to seek payment for the Indians who were feeding them.” He brought back with him his cousin, M. Lascaris* d’Urfé. Instead of spending the winter of 1669 with h
2 CONC is two companions, he went to teach the Indians of Gandaseteiagon in their village located on Lake Ontario near the present Port Hope.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was not to be expected that these missionaries would themselves provide a detailed account of their heroic enterprise. In 1669, when Bishop Laval wanted to publish the story of their exploits in the Jesuit Relations, M. de Féne
2 CONC lon made him this reply: “The greatest favour that you can grant us is not to have us mentioned at all.” Nevertheless, in 1672 M. Dollier* de Casson appended to his Histoire du Montréal a long letter written by M. Trouvé, which is
2 CONC  a résumé of the history of the Kenté mission. One can glimpse in it the great daring and stamina which characterized these athletic young missionaries who propelled their birch-bark canoes through rapids and ice floes as they tra
2 CONC velled from Lake Ontario to Montreal and Quebec, wintering in the woods where at times they got lost, eating sagamité and pumpkin, sharing the wretchedness of the Indians, and succeeding only in baptizing children or a few adults 
2 CONC on the point of death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fortunately there was interest in the Kenté mission in Paris, and it was decided to begin building there and to send out some animals, which was done in the course of the following decade.
2 CONT
2 CONT At Montreal there was concern about the education of Indian children. It was thought necessary to move them away from the town, and consideration was given, as a site for settling them, to three islands in Lac Saint-Louis (above L
2 CONC achine) which were given the name Gentilly. M. de Fénelon was summoned there, as he already had experience of Indian life. He arranged to have these islands granted to him in due form. On that occasion Governor Buade de Frontenac 
2 CONC wrote, 9 Jan. 1673: “The great zeal that Sieur Abbé de Fénelon has exhibited for several years in the propagation of Christianity in this colony, and the devotion that he has displayed in His Majesty’s service, constrain us to see
2 CONC k every kind of means of recognizing them and of pressing him to keep up the zeal he has shown up to the present; a zeal whose ardour has prompted him to abandon all the substantial establishments that his birth and merit might ha
2 CONC ve entitled him to expect in France, in order to devote himself entirely to the conversion and education of the Indians.” On the shores of the island adjacent to the lake the seminary of Montreal erected the necessary buildings, i
2 CONC n the hope of attracting some new settlers to this part of the island. Soon M. d’Urfé came to rejoin his colleague there.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1674 we find M. de Fénelon at Montreal again. By virtue of his personal qualities and his name he was in the good graces of the governors of Quebec and Montreal, Frontenac and Perrot. But this situation was soon to change.
2 CONT
2 CONT François-Marie Perrot, who had married Talon’s niece and whom M. de Bretonvilliers, superior of the seminary of Saint-Sulpice in Paris, had appointed governor of Montreal at the request of the intendant, was not the personage one 
2 CONC might have hoped for. He had come to Canada only to make his fortune and had soon revealed himself in his true light: grasping, arrogant, having no respect for the seigneurs, and taking improper advantage of his office; he had ope
2 CONC ned, on the island that bears his name, a trading agency, whose management he entrusted to his clerk, Antoine de La Frenaye de Brucy, and, scorning the regulations, he authorized frequent leaves for the coureurs de bois. Frontenac
2 CONC  for his part, no less eager for gain, had used corvée labour to construct Fort Cataracoui (Frontenac), in order to protect and group the Indians; he made use of the fort, however, to carry on trade in pelts through the intermedia
2 CONC ry of his liegeman, Robert Cavelier de La Salle, to whom he had given the post. Now it happened that two outlawed coureurs de bois came to Montreal and sought seclusion in the home of M. de Carion, a confidant of Perrot’s. The jud
2 CONC ge of Montreal, Charles d’Ailleboust, wanted to seize them. Perrot resisted this. The judge wrote to Frontenac, who dispatched Bizard. The latter was coolly received by Perrot, although it must be said that Bizard had behaved in v
2 CONC ery provocative fashion and had done so on Frontenac’s orders. The governor gave Perrot notice to appear before him at Quebec and wrote to M. de Fénelon, their mutual friend, asking him to act as his intermediary. Fénelon prevaile
2 CONC d upon Perrot and they both went down to Quebec. Upon their arrival Frontenac imprisoned Perrot, and Fénelon, in attempting to intercede for him, annoyed the governor and lost his friendship. Fénelon had realized, moreover, that F
2 CONC rontenac had taken advantage of his friendship and had deceived him.
2 CONT
2 CONT On his return to Montreal, the abbé thought it discreet, in view of probable future difficulties, to turn over to the seminary his property holdings on the Îles Gentilly, where the seminary had, after all, met all the expenses. Fé
2 CONC nelon, who was responsible for delivering the sermon at Easter high mass, preached to all the citizens of Montreal. In the second part of his sermon, dealing with the duties of those who are set in authority, he alluded to certain
2 CONC  abuses, especially to burdensome corvées, and so on. La Salle created an uproar at that point; he stood up and drew the attention of the hearers to what the preacher was saying. The general opinion was that in his criticisms the 
2 CONC abbé had been alluding to Governor Frontenac.
2 CONT
2 CONT After mass all M. de Fénelon’s colleagues condemned his sermon. The superior went to make his apologies to the local commandant, M. de Lanouguère, and wrote to the governor himself to dissociate the Society from the blunder commit
2 CONC ted by one of its members. Frontenac took a very high and mighty attitude and requested the superior to expel M. de Fénelon from the Society. (It should be pointed out that Sulpicians do not take any vow of obedience.) M. de Fénel
2 CONC on went away, however, of his own accord and carried on his ministry at Lachine.
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition to his sermon on 25 March 1674, the abbé had committed another indiscretion: that of having the citizens of Montreal sign a petition protesting against the arbitrary imprisonment of Perrot at Quebec. Hence Frontenac or
2 CONC dered him to appear before him. M. de Fénelon went to Quebec and challenged the right of the Conseil Souverain to judge him. These scions of ancient families, proud of their noble birth, did not fear to speak bluntly before their 
2 CONC peers. Furthermore, the suit that was being brought against Abbé Fénelon was contrary to both the ecclesiastical and the civil laws of the kingdom. Despite the intervention of Lascaris d’Urfé, a relative of the abbé and a friend o
2 CONC f Frontenac, matters became so unpleasant that the Conseil Souverain decided to refer the litigation to the king himself. M. d’Urfé, for his part, sent off a conclusive report to the minister, Colbert.
2 CONT
2 CONT Perrot and Fénelon went to France in 1674. Perrot was shut up in the Bastille for some months and then sent back to his governor’s duties at Montreal. Frontenac was very severely reprimanded by the king for his attitude towards M.
2 CONC  de Fénelon. It was solely to safeguard the governor’s authority that the king refrained from publicity rebuking him. As a result of this episode, the members of the Conseil Souverain were thenceforth appointed by the king.
2 CONT
2 CONT As for M. de Fénelon, forbidden by the king to remain in Canada, denounced by the seminary of Paris just as he had been by that of Montreal, he withdrew from the Society of Saint-Sulpice.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 7 May 1675 M. de Bretonvilliers said to the priests of Montreal: “I urge you all to profit from the example of M. de Fénelon. By dint of too much intriguing in society and interfering in what did not concern him, he has marred 
2 CONC all his undertakings and damaged those of his friends while trying to serve them. In matters of this sort, which are concerned only with private differences, neutrality will always be the desirable course.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Where did M. de Fénelon spend his retirement? Historians have so far been unable to find out. Was it with his uncle, the bishop of Sarlat, or, more likely, on the estate of his family in Dordogne? We know only that he died in 1679
2 CONC  at the age of 38.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is a matter of regret that the career of this fearless missionary was so soon broken off. Not even a trace remains of the mission he founded at Kenté. Only his name lingers on, at Fenelon Falls on the Trent River.
2 CONT
2 CONT Olivier Maurault
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivanhoë Caron, “Inventaire de documents,” APQ Rapport, 1939–40, 221–25. Correspondance de Frontenac (1672–82), APQ Rapport, 1926–27, 67–73, 81. Dollier de Casson, Histoire du Montréal. Jug. et délib. “Le proces de l’abbé de Fénelo
2 CONC n devant le Conseil souverain de la Nouvelle-France en 1674,” APQ Rapport, 1921–22, 124–88. “Le différend entre M. de Frontenac et l’abbé de Fénelon,” BRH, XLII (1936), 614–17. Eccles, Frontenac. Faillon, Histoire de la colonie fr
2 CONC ançaise, III. Aegidius Fauteux, “Les surprises de la généalogie,” BRH, LI (1945), 391–94. Lionel Groulx, “Frontenac vs l’abbé de Fénelon: une tragi-comédie judiciaire,” RHAF, XII (1958–59), 358–71.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT comte de La Mothe-Fénelon, vicomte de Saint-Julien du Lampon, baron de Loubert et de Manot, seigneur de Boisse, seigneur de Péricart(etc), seigneur de Fénelon(Sainte-Mondane 24)
2 CONT
2 CONT Il habitait le château de Fénelon, paroisse de Saint-Mondane.
