2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fran%C3%A7oise_Marie_de_Bourbon Françoise Marie de Bourbon From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Jump to: navigation, search Françoise Marie Légitimée de France Duchess of Orléans First Princess of the
2 CONC  Blood Françoise Marie by Alexandre François Caminade, 1834, painted i the reign of her descendant Louis Philippe I Spouse 	Philippe d'Orléans, Duke of Orléans Detail Issue Louise Élisabeth, Duchess of Berry Adélaïde, Abbess of Ch
2 CONC elles Charlotte Aglaé, Duchess of Modena Louis, Duke of Orléans Élisabeth, Queen of Spain Philippine Élisabeth, Mademoiselle de Beaujolais Louise, Princess of Conti. Full name Françoise Marie de Bourbon, Légitimée de France Royal 
2 CONC 	House of Orléans House of Bourbon Father 	Louis XIV of France Mother 	Françoise-Athénaïs de Mortemart, Marchioness of Montespan Born 	4 May 1677(1677-05-04) Château de Maintenon, France Died 	1 February 1749 (aged 71) Palais Roya
2 CONC l, Paris, France Burial 	6 February 1749[1] Église de la Madeleine de Trainel, Paris, France
2 CONT
2 CONT Françoise Marie de Bourbon, Légitimée de France (4 May[2] 1677 – 1 February 1749) was the youngest legitimised daughter (fille légitimée de France) of Louis XIV of France and his maîtresse-en-titre, Françoise-Athénaïs, marquise de
2 CONC  Montespan. Originally known as the second Mademoiselle de Blois[3], that style eventually gave way to the name Françoise Marie de Blois. She married her first cousin Philippe d'Orléans in 1692 at the age of fourteen and produced 
2 CONC some eight children, four of which would have further progeny[3].
2 CONT
2 CONT Proud, lazy and attractive, she successfully intrigued to have her daughters marry well to the Prince of Conti, Duke of Berry, Duke of Modena and King of Spain, mostly to spite her sister Louise Françoise de Bourbon, the Duchess o
2 CONC f Bourbon. However, she welded little political influence considering her proximity to the political circle of the era. She was involved in the Cellamare Conspiracy in 1718 which was supposed to overthrow her own husband Philippe 
2 CONC d'Orléans as Regent of the Kingdom and replace him with her favourite brother the Duke of Maine. The marquis d'Argenson said she was very like her mother, Madame de Montespan, but also had Louis XIV's orderly mind with his failing
2 CONC s of injustice and harshness[3].
2 CONT
2 CONT Among her male line descendents are Philippe Egalité, Louis-Philippe I, King of the French, and Prince Henri, Count of Paris, the present Orléanist pretender to the French throne. She is also an ancestor of Juan Carlos I of Spain,
2 CONC  Albert II, King of the Belgians, Henri, Grand Duke of Luxembourg and Vittorio Emanuele, Prince of Naples, the pretender to the Italian throne. Contents [show]
2 CONT
