1 OCCU Commissioner for Argyllshire, and later MP for Dunbartonshire.
1 TITL Honourable
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Family and Education b. c.1660, 2nd s. of Archibald, 8th Earl of Argyll [S], by Lady Mary Stuart, da. of James, 4th Earl of Moray [S]. educ. Glasgow 1676. m. 1692, Elizabeth, da. of John, 8th Lord Elphinstone [S], 
2 CONC 7s. 5da.
2 CONT
2 CONT Offices Held
2 CONT
2 CONT M.P. [S] Argyllshire 1700-7; surveyor of the King’s works [S], 1705-17.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography Uncle of the 2nd and 3rd and father of the 4th Duke of Argyll, Campbell was re-elected in 1715 for Dunbartonshire, which he had represented as a Whig since the Union. After voting for the septennial bill in 1716, he foll
2 CONC owed Argyll into opposition, losing his place in 1717, but supporting the Government in 1719, when Argyll returned to office. Defeated in 1722 but awarded the seat on petition, he stood down in favour of his son John in 1727. He d
2 CONC ied 9 Apr. 1729.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref Volumes: 1715-1754 Author: J. M. Simpson
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The Hon. John Campbell (c. 1660 – 9 April 1729), of Mamore, was a Scottish Whig politician who sat in the Parliament of Scotland from 1700 to 1707 and in the British House of Commons between 1708 and 1727.
2 CONT
2 CONT Campbell was the second son of Archibald Campbell, 9th Earl of Argyll and his wife Lady Mary Stewart, daughter of the 4th Earl of Moray.[1] He was educated at Glasgow in 1676. Being party to his father's unsuccessful rising agains
2 CONC t James II, he suffered forfeiture and banishment and was always in financial difficulties. He served as Captain of foot in the Earl of Argyll's regiment from 1689 until after 1690. In 1692 he married Elizabeth Elphinstone, the da
2 CONC ughter of John, 8th Lord Elphinstone[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Campbell was Commissioner for Argyllshire in the Parliament of Scotland from 1700 to 1707 and surveyor of the King's works in Scotland from 1705 to 1717.[3] He supported the Union of Scotland and England and after the Union in 170
2 CONC 7 was considered sound enough to represent Scotland in the British Parliament that year. At the 1708 general election, he was returned unopposed as Member of Parliament (MP) for Dunbartonshire. He was returned unopposed again in 1
2 CONC 710 when he was classed as a Whig. He made little impression in Parliament, being often absent. He was returned unopposed again at the 1713 general election.[2] He was returned again at the 1715 general election and voted for the 
2 CONC septennial bill in 1716. He went into opposition with the Duke of Argyll and lost his official post in 1717.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1719 the Duke of Argyll returned to office and Campbell changed his support to the government. In 1722 there was a contest at Dumbarton in which he was defeated in the poll. He was returned on petition on 23 January 1725 and he
2 CONC ld the seat until the 1727 general election. He then resigned his seat in favour of his son John.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Campbell died on 9 April 1729. He and his wife had issue:
2 CONT
2 CONT # John Campbell, 4th Duke of Argyll
2 CONT # Charles Campbell of Auchnacreive, MP for Argyllshire
2 CONT # Neil Campbell
2 CONT # William Campbell
2 CONT # Mary Campbell, married James Primrose, 2nd Earl of Rosebery
2 CONT # Anne Campbell, married Archibald Edmonstone of Duntreath, had issue:
2 CONT # Sir Archibald Edmonstone, 1st Baronet
2 CONT # Isabella Campbell, married Captain William Montgomery of Rosemount
2 CONT # Jean Campbell, married Captain John Campbell of Carrick
2 CONT # Primrose Cambell, married Simon Fraser, 11th Lord Lovat
2 CONT # Elizabeth Campbell
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT #  Donald C. V. Campbell, "Campbell, Duke of Argyll" in Sir James Balfour Paul ed., The Scots Peerage, volume I (Edinburgh, 1904) p. 381.
2 CONT #  "CAMPBELL, Hon. John (c.1660-1729), of Mamore, Dunbarton". History of Parliament Online (1690-1715). Retrieved 9 October 2018.
2 CONT #  Foster, Joseph, ed. (1882), Members of Parliament, Scotland 1357-1882, Hazell, Watson and Viney, retrieved 21 June 2019
2 CONT #  "CAMPBELL, Hon. John (c.1660-1729), of Mamore, Dunbarton". History of Parliament Online (1715-1754). Retrieved 3 April 2013.
2 CONT
