1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == Wikipedia Biographical Summary ==
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Erskine_of_Carnock John Erskine of Carnock] (4 November 1695 – 1 March 1768) was a Scottish jurist and professor of Scottish law at the University of Edinburgh. He wrote the Principles of the Law
2 CONC  of Scotland and An Institute of the Law of Scotland, prominent books on Scots law.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Background ===
2 CONT A member of Clan Erskine, Erskine was the son of John Erskine of Cardross, who had made his fortune joining the army of William of Orange which invaded England in 1688. His grandfather was David Erskine, 2nd Lord Cardross, while h
2 CONC is mother, Anna, the daughter and heiress of Sir William Dundas of Kincavel, was his father's second wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Legal career ===
2 CONT Erskine was educated for the law and joined the Faculty of Advocates in 1719 and followed the career of an Advocate for some years, apparently with no obvious distinction. However, the post of Professor of Scots Law at Edinburgh U
2 CONC niversity became vacant in 1737, with the death of its incumbent Alexander Bain. Edinburgh University is a modern (that is, a post-Reformation foundation, so the appointment of professors lay with the Town Council, which asked the
2 CONC  Faculty of Advocates to suggest two names. The second was normally one who was certain to refuse, so the fact that John Erskine was the other nominee shows the esteem in which he was now held in the profession (though no doubt hi
2 CONC s connections in the Erskine and Dundas families also played their part).
2 CONT
2 CONT Erskine was allowed £100 per annum along with student fees. Seemingly, his lectures were very popular and he decided to produce an up-to-date text for his students to replace James Dalrymple, 1st Viscount of Stair's much used Inst
2 CONC itutions of the Law of Scotland. Erskine wished to expand on the brevity of Mackenzie' work, fill in gaps and bring it up to date. Erskine's own work, Principles of the Law of Scotland, bore the imprint of Mackenzie's book in its 
2 CONC organisation, though in 8 volumes, appeared in 1754. It has been in print ever since.
2 CONT
2 CONT Erskine retired from the professorship in 1765 and devoted himself to a new work "An Institute of the Law of Scotland." He had substantially completed this when he died on 1 March 1768, but had not brought it to full completion. T
2 CONC his was done by "a legal friend" in consultation with other advocates. It was obviously part of one of the great Enlightenment Projects - namely the systematisation of the law. This was no mean task, but its success can be judged 
2 CONC from the fact that it has never been out of print - though always revised - since its publication in 1773. It had, and has, its critics - there are places where its posthumous publication is all too apparent, and many felt it was 
2 CONC far too academical (with a poor feel for the daily practice of law) but it quickly gained a reputation as a reliable reference point and, probably, no book has been cited as frequently in Scottish courts.
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.familysearch.org/en/tree/person/97MJ-12L
2 CONT
2 CONT === Family ===
2 CONT Erskine was twice married. He married as his first wife Margaret, daughter of James Melville, in 1719. Their son was the influential theologian John Erskine. He married as his second wife Anne, daughter of James Sterling of Keir, 
2 CONC in 1729. Several descendants of his son from this marriage, James Erskine, gained distinction, including John Erskine, Sir David Erskine, Sir Arthur Erskine, Sir James Erskine and Sir George Erskine. Erskine died in March 1768, ag
2 CONC ed 72.
2 CONT
2 CONT >SOURCE: Wikipedia contributors, 'John Erskine of Carnock', Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia, 16 November 2012, 02:34 UTC, <http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=John_Erskine_of_Carnock&oldid=523259477> [accessed 16 July 2013]
2 CONT == Other References ==
2 CONT * Burke, Bernard, Sir.  [https://archive.org/stream/genealogicalhera01byuburk#page/518/mode/2up A genealogical and heraldic history of the landed gentry of Great Britain & Ireland 6th ed]. London : Harrison 1879. Vol I. page 518
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT A Scottish jurist, Professor of Law at Edinburgh University and author of the standard text on Scottish law, Principles of the Law of Scotland, 
