1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Sources:'''
2 CONT [https://www.nosorigines.qc.ca/GenealogieQuebec.aspx?genealogie=Rigaud_Judith&pid=6630 Nosorigines]
2 CONT [https://gw.geneanet.org/ottopalfenier?lang=en&p=judith&n=rigaud Geneanet]
2 CONT
2 CONT CT 24-02 soul) with François Lemaître said LOHDaughter of Elisha and Suzanne Dugast. Committed the Rochelle in 1651 by Marguerite LeGardeur. Remarried in 1667 to Jean Therrienremarried in 1775 with Jean Laplanchemarried 1686 to St
2 CONC -Saint-Jean-d ' Angély in FranceArrested 1679 under the charge of deserting the matrimonial home and live as man and wife with a named Pierre Cavelier and condammnee to 10 years of banishment from the island of Montreal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of course, everyone knows that our ancestors "canayens" were "pious" and "submitted to the authorities. that they are religious, civil, or even military.So here is the story of a remarkable woman, wife of the pioneer François Lema
2 CONC ître said Lamorille, daughter of the King, called: Judith Rigaud.We will discover the nature of these brave women until the temerity — that were our arrears-arrears-grand-mothers and who raised our ancestors.At the age of 23 years
2 CONC , Judith agrees to emigrate to New France. She entered the service of Jacques Leneuf the pottery of Trois-Rivières and his wife, Marguerite Legardeur.Trois-Rivières, in 1651, is a few dozen families refuge inside a small fort, log
2 CONC s, most of the time surrounded by Iroquois marauders.Judith is beautiful, introspective, soup to the milk and has no respect for social and moral conventions. It is not those who conceal their nature. She loves life and its benefi
2 CONC ts, and openly mocks those who venture to criticize.Eventually, she falls under the spell of one of the soldiers of the garrison of Trois-Rivières, a certain François Lemaître said Lamorille said Picard. It is a famous talker, an 
2 CONC inveterate drinker and an avid 'player'. In other words, it is very popular socially.The guests at their wedding list resembles that of the 'Jet Set' of the new France of the 17th century. The most famous of the guests is Médard C
2 CONC houart Groseillers, arrived in the country since 1641 and husband of Hélène Martin since 1647. Médard, in 1657, in the company of Pierre-Esprit Radisson, arrived in Quebec with an incredible cargo of pelts, after an expedition in 
2 CONC the pays d'en haut which lasted three years. He thus saved the colony from bankruptcy. It will do so again in 1660; but will seize its cargo of 100 fur trade canoes.Judith's relationship with his employers are sparks and disintegr
2 CONC ate quickly. One of different ends with Judith who, filled with anger, begins to break furniture. In June 1654, Ms. Legardeur and the Livingston/Rigaud clash in court of justice because Judith refuses to complete his five-year con
2 CONC tract, signed in La Rochelle three years earlier.About François Lemaître, he made many waves in society. It appears in more than 20 court cases, either in defense or prosecution, between the years 1654-1666. Treated cases are libe
2 CONC l, assault or 'gambling '.Judith is obliged to repay the Le Gardeur to a sum of 102 pounds for "breach of contract", while they must give him his clothes "seized".Judith Rigaud isn't, you can guess, a shy and silent wife. It is ve
2 CONC ry well suited, as the other "filles du Roi" of the colony, conditions that require courage, character, work and tenacity.Most of the new France women are more educated than men and they represent themselves, to lead the small bus
2 CONC inesses of their spouses who import / export or haggle over clothes, furs, brandy and utensils. This "bargain" often includes smuggling, but no one dares admit, even today.Both sexes of our colony are responsible for clearing work
2 CONC  and all, without exception, know how to wield the axe, the musket and rifle. Very few women, however, have what is called: 'wardrobe', except... Judith.Under the guidance of his friend from des Groseilliers, François Lemaître emb
2 CONC arks on the fur trade. The profits allow it to buy clothes and furniture for the family. The couple has eight children, all born in Trois-Rivières, except Charles said Lemaître Auger which, later a personality of his time.October 
2 CONC 25, 1655, François Lemaître bought a House on St-Pierre Street in Trois-Rivières. Two years later, the couple bought is another land of 2 acres by 25 to 150 pounds.In 1660, they rent yet another land on the banks of the St-Maurice
2 CONC , and in November, François pays 200 pounds for a property called 'Marguerite'.The family business is progressing and François becomes little by little an important character in her community.In 1666, is Judith Rigaud which takes 
2 CONC the exclusive management of the company of her husband therefore in the following events:In summer 1665, Judith Rigaud returned to La Rochelle, for, it seems, establishing trade agreements with suppliers. Travel overseas frightene
2 CONC d not at all our ancestors. whether they are men or women.A historian suggests that she decided to leave her husband to visit relatives and set a legacy, while making the remark that François continues to accumulate debts and drin
2 CONC k with excess. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In her absence her husband was murdered.  The children were taken in by their uncle, Antoine LaMaitre dit Lamorille.  Rigaud quickly found a new husband, Jean Therrien du Ponceau dit Duhaime, and proceeded to sell assets to settle
2 CONC  a huge debt.  
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1670 Judith was a widow again.  Her husband died in an accident during a trading expedition in the fall of that year.  Left with three small Therrien sons she remarried a third time, in 1675, to Jean LaPlanche, a surgeon who re
2 CONC cently arrived in New France.  
2 CONT
2 CONT In early 1676 Judith's daughter, Marie LeMaitre, married Jacques Passard, sieur de la Brettonniere, a censitaire at Riviere-du-Loup.  Passard was a veteran of the DuGue Company of the Carignan-Saliers Regiment. He entered the fur 
2 CONC trade with two other former soldiers, Paul Guyon dit Letremblade of the La Fouille Company and Christophe Gerbaud dit Bellgarde, another La Fouille veteran who had also married a daughter of Judith and settled in Riviere-du-Loop. 
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1678 Jean Laplanche had returned to France after Judith Regaud, his wife, had deserted him to live with Pierre Cavelier, a man to whom he had leased some farm land.  The illicit couple was hounded by creditors whose agents were
2 CONC  threatened and chased off the farm by Judith wielding a pitchfork.  In 1679 the scandalized authorities in Montreal banished Judith from Montreal for ten years on pain of corporal punishment.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Judith prudently left Montreal to live on the Seigneury at Riviere du Loop. She resumed her fur trading activities in concert with her sons, and her sons in law, who had as much difficulty staying on the right side of the law as J
2 CONC udith.  In 1681 Jacques Rigard and Christophe Gerbaud dit Bellegarde were both arrested by the authorities for illegally trading with the Indians.  They were fined 200.00 pounds and had their weapons and canoes confiscated and sol
2 CONC d.  This did not deter the two who purchased provisions from the merchant Joseph Petit dit Bruno and carried on with the trade shortly thereafter.  Later Gerbaud dit Bellegarde entered a clandestine partnership with Petit dit Brun
2 CONC o, Medard Chouart des Groseilliers, Pierre Esprit Raddison and other prominent merchants to trade furs illegally in the Gaspe Peninsula, launching their vessels from the coast of France.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Because of Judith's multiple marriages and her considerable involvement in the fur trade, few, if any, of the censitaires at Riviere du Loop were free of her family's influence.  Most of the residents of the Siegneury, including t
2 CONC he de Gerlaise family, became connected to the LeMaitres by marriage as time went on.  The commercial opportunities in the fur trade created by Judith and her associates offered a source of extra prosperity, if not notariety, for 
2 CONC the community as a whole.  
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.duhaime.org/DuhaimeFamilyHistory/Chapter2FillesduRoy.aspx
2 CONT
2 CONT Judith is mentioned in the book "The People who Own Themselves: Aboriginal Ethnogenesis in a Canadian Family ..." by Heather Divine.
2 CONT
