1 NOTE Additional reference: 50-6.
2 CONT Jette pg 489; 650; 703.
2 CONT Tanguay Vol I pg 374; Vol V pg 309.
2 CONT "Our French Canadian Ancestors"-Laforest Vol IV Chap 17 and 18.
2 CONT "1667 Census of Quebec" Vol II pg 79.
2 CONT "Emigration Rochelaise en Nouvelle France"-Godbout pg 148-149.
2 CONT "The Great Families" from the internet (copy filed 50-2).
2 CONT <http://home.attbi.com/~sodapop68/webtree/pafg25.htm> (copy filed 50-2).
2 CONT LDS Ancestral File, group record of self (#1469), (copy filed 50-2).
2 CONT Drouin pg 822.
2 CONT Marriage contract 24-02 Ameau.
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------
2 CONT Judith Rigaud, born in France about 1633/36.  On 22 June 1651 she contracted 5 years to Marguerite Gardeur (spouse of Jacques LeNeuf #894) at Trois-Rivieres, St Maurice, Quebec, Canada.  While still under contract, she married 6 M
2 CONC ay 1654 to Francois Lemaitre.  The breach of contract was settled by a payment to Marguerite Gardeur for the unserved time of the contract.  Her marriage to Francois Lemaitre produced 8 children, the last child born after the deat
2 CONC h of Francois, who was killed by the Iroquois.  His burial record dated 14 Jan 1666 is shown in the records of Immaculee Conception Church at Trois-Rivieres (have copy of this record).  Prior to the death of her spouse, who was pr
2 CONC one to heavy drinking, she left him and returned to France.  Unknown to her at the time, she was pregnant with child (Charles) who was born 15 Apr 1666 in France.  Judith returned to Canada in the summer of 1666 and was jolted by 
2 CONC the news that her spouse had died and was heavily indebted. This debt was settled in Aug of 1668.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tanguay Vol V pg 309 shows Judith remarried 26 Jan 1667 at Immaculee Conception Church, Trois-Rivieres (have copy of this record) to Jean Terrien (aka Jean Duhèmme).  Jean died about 1670.  Two children were born of this marriage 
2 CONC although "Jette" pg 1072 shows 3 children.  The children were: Dominique, bapt. 6 Nov 1667 and buried 6 Dec 1667 (have copy of these records); Jean, born 16 and bapt. 17 Mar 1669 (have copy of this record); married 1700 to Marguer
2 CONC ite Laspron.  These records are in the register of Immaculee Conception Church, Trois-Rivieres.  The third child (Louis Michel) was born 19 Mar 1671 and appears in the records of Immaculee Conception Church, Trois-Rivieres dated 5
2 CONC  Apr 1671.  This bapt. record indicates some confusion as to the name of the father.  It is assumed that the child was born out of wedlock but was known as Louis Michel Terrien.  According to "Our French Canadian Ancestors"-Lamber
2 CONC t, the father of this child was Jean Duhèmme (this name is the aka of Jean Terrien).  The name Jean Duhèmme does appear on the baptism register.  Just preceeding his name on the register there is an area that is heavily scratched 
2 CONC out and not legible.  Could this have been another name? (have copy of this record).
2 CONT
2 CONT Judith again remarried 6 Oct 1675 at Immaculee Conception Church, Trois-Rivieres (have copy of this record) to Jean de Laplanche who was the first resident surgeon of Riviere-du-Loup, Louiseville, Maskinonge, Quebec, Canada.  This
2 CONC  marriage is listed on pg 650 of "Jette".  In 1679 Judith was arrested and accused of deserting her family and living with another man named Pierre Cavelier.  Judith was banned from Montreal for 10 years because of this action.  H
2 CONC er husband, Jean de Laplanche returned to France, leaving his unfaithful wife in June of 1678.  Pierre Cavelier also abandoned her to take a legitimate wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is believed that Judith returned to Louiseville and appears on the 1681 census as Judith Desauneaux. living with her son-in-law, Guillaume Christophe Gerbault, who married Judith's daughter Marguerite Lemaitre.  (see "Jette" pg
2 CONC  489 for this family.)
2 CONT
2 CONT A fourth marriage for Judith is shown on LDS Family Registry #39534 dated 6 Feb 1686 to Louis Gillet.  Intensive search was made to find a hint of this marriage, but nothing was found to confirm or deny same.  Tanguay Vol I pg 374
2 CONC  shows another marriage dated 30 July 1690 to Jacques Abelin at Ste Anne.   Could not locate any proof of this event.  However, LDS IGI of Quebec as of 1992 shows this Jacques Abelin married to Marie Gasnier (aka Gagnon) at Ste An
2 CONC ne on this date.  Could Judith have used another alias?
2 CONT
2 CONT Judith died 13 May 1703 according to LDS Ancestral File, group record of self (copy filed 50-2).
2 CONT
2 CONT It is to be noted, that in several of the articles about her, that she was apparently well educated and had a very good handwriting.
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://www.google.com/search?sourceid=navclient&q=%22judith+rigaud%22> a translated version follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT Live the love
2 CONT
2 CONT Judith Rigaud, a girl to be married, enters to the service of Marguerite LeGardeur, son of Jacques LeNeuf of the Pottery, about 1652. She is originating in Midsummer's Day d'Angely, in Charente-Maritime.
2 CONT
2 CONT Remaining at Three-Rivers, she succumbs to the charm, undoubtedly exceptional, of François Lemaistre aka Lamorille 23 years old from Picardy, which, with the quality of a fine talker, and a heavy drinker!
2 CONT
2 CONT Lemaistre marries the his beauty 6 May 1654, for best or worst. Seven children are born under the lenient sky from Three-Rivers while Lemaistre accumulates the debts and ruins his health. In 1665, Judith Rigaud must go to France, 
2 CONC to undoubtedly regulate there some business problems of succession. It is probable qu?au moment of its departure, Judith is unaware of qu?elle is "in an interesting state". What qu?il is, Charles, last child of the couple, is born
2 CONC  at Saint-Jean-de-la-Rochelle  where he is baptized, April 15, 1666.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between the diapers and the business of her husband, Judith Rigaud engages personally to refund the debts of which he was jusqu?àlors the only person in charge. She poses this generous gesture, "believing to find her husband livin
2 CONC g" and of the goods in sufficient quality and quantity to assume the expenditure of some effects bought with Amault Perré and intended to be resold here. The return in boat will be without pity for the herds and the goods bought i
2 CONC n France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another disappointment: Judith learns while going down from boat, with he summer of 1666, that Lemaistre is no more, having been buried on previous 14 January. But Judith, who is now the head of a family of eight children, still h
2 CONC as many charms: January 26, 1667, she convoles with Jean Terrien with whom she will have two other children. One does not know when this second husband dies, however, Judith contracts a third union, October 6, 1675, with the surge
2 CONC on Jean de LaPlanche, first doctor to be resided at the River-of-Wolf (today Louiseville).
2 CONT
2 CONT This marriage n?est not of happiest. In 1677, Judith Rigaud abondonné the marital home and emigrant in Montreal where, without contract nor blessing, she lives under the same roof as Pierre Cavelier. At the 15th century, in New-Fr
2 CONC ance, the cohabitation is rather badly carried. It is undoubtedly why the sovereign Council hesitated to stop Judith under the accusation of having abandoned the marital home. With the social vexations that the financial vexations
2 CONC  that the woman already knows well.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Robert-Lionel Séguin, "marked publicly that she was the concubine of Cavelier, Judith Rigaud will manage to be rehabilitated opposite the public opinion and to finish her days respected by her neighbors".
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Our p180 roots
