2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://iquebec.ifrance.com/leotherrien/article3.html>.  It contained a copy of the contract of marriage of Jean Terrien and Judith Rigaud and could not be trannlated.  Acopy of this contract is fil
2 CONC ed in 50-3.
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://iquebec.ifrance.com/leotherrien/article2.html> a translated version follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT The Therrien ancestor and the fur trade
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of a meeting of the members, the autumn passed, we advanced that the consultation of the texts published in the reviews was significant to locate the bonds with the people surrounding our ancestors.
2 CONT
2 CONT At this time, we had spoken about Marguerite FLYING, girl of Claude STEALING sior of Saint-Claude, of unknown origin, then commercial with Three-Rivers ( Jetté , p.1131), deceased about 1690. It married about 1653 with Three-River
2 CONC s with Francoise RADISSON, girl of Pierre-Spirit and Madeleine HÉNAULT, Saint-Sulpice in Paris. The Jetté dictionary quotes nine children, of which this Francoise Radisson, sister of Élisabeth woman of Claude JUTRAS and half-siste
2 CONC r of Marguerite HAYET, woman of Jean MINNOW.
2 CONT
2 CONT By examining the names of the people present at the marriage of LAND Jean and Judith RUGAUD, we could find the ground of the ancestor who is located at the corner of theSaint-Maurice streets and Saint-Paul with Three-Rivers (1).
2 CONT
2 CONT Faithful so that we advance in the review Therrien (vol.1, no.1, p.14), we always affirm, and with more conviction still, that LAND Jean and Judith RIGAUD have bonds with the trade of the furs .
2 CONT
2 CONT The father of LAND Jean was a merchant-clothier : here are which predisposes with the trade of the fur in News-France. By consulting the dictionary of Rene Jetté and by raising the various ancestors and individuals from Europe, it
2 CONC  is easy to locate people according to their trade, and it is relevant also to gather them by area of origin; that leads us to outline the networks of relations and to look further into our study on the subject.
2 CONT
2 CONT Before leaning us on the people quoted with the marriage contract, we must also check the way in which the individuals wrote.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sailor TERRIE(R) - perhaps "N" - sior of Repentigny and Francheville, originating in Saint Martin's day of Large-Mesnil, is évêché of Lisieux in Normandy ( Jetté , p.1066); soldier killed by Iroquois with Three-Rivers on August 19
2 CONC , 1652, it married in Quebec on September 3, 1647 (ct. Tronquet) with Jeanne JALEAU says Ploumelle, of Fontenay-the-Count, Poitou. The latter was the maidservant of Pierre LEGARDEUR in 1647, it was remariée in Maurice POULIN in 16
2 CONC 54 and had the concession of theSaint-Maurice seigniory on August 14, 1676, yielded to his/her children on January 19, 1683. LAND sailor [? ] and Jeanne JALEAU were pilot with the marriage of LAND Jean and Judith RIGAUD.
2 CONT
2 CONT Another fact concerning the LEGARDEUR: Marguerite LEGARDEUR (always according to Jetté, p.695) also came from Normandy. Pierre, the brother of Marguerite, is deceased in May 1648 while returning from the LaRochelle, and Ignace, hi
2 CONC s son, signs "sior of the Culvert": he was born with Our-Lady-of-Knocks (La Rochelle). To notice that LAND Jean also signs "sior of the Culvert".
2 CONT
2 CONT Which was not our astonishment, by raising the names of the witnesses to the promise of marriage of LAND Jean (Dieppe) and Judith RIGAUD (Saint-Jean-Angely), to find there a range of the high society of Three-Rivers...
2 CONT
2 CONT Names that several have as ancestors who are quoted in an article of the Memories of the French Canadian genealogical company (2) by Hubert Charbonneau, deputy manager of the Research Programme in Historical Demography .
2 CONT
2 CONT The BASTION family is another military and commercial family . The author says that BASTON took again the trade of merchant , it is also quoted like soldier. This last collaborates with Daniel BRAILLE, a merchant calvinist - anoth
2 CONC er bond which we seek for the baptism of LAND Pierre (La Rochelle) and Gabrielle MIGNOT [ MINEAU ] (Fontenay-the-Count).
2 CONT
2 CONT This same Simon BASTON is not found with the census of 1666, just like LAND Jean and Judith RIGAUD. Simon BASTON is seen on the incoming vessel of France in 1663. And at the beginning of October 1665, it sets out again in France w
2 CONC ith a great quantity of skins being worth several thousands of books; it dies in enigmatic circumstances with the autumn of 1673.
2 CONT
