1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * [http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean-Baptiste_Donatien_de_Vimeur_de_Rochambeau Jean-Baptiste Donatien de Vimeur, comte de Rochambeau], né le 1er juillet 1725 à Vendôme et mort le 10 mai 1807 à Thoré-la-Rochette, es
2 CONC t un militaire français des XVIIIe et XIXe siècles. Il s'illustre à la tête du corps expéditionnaire français lors de la guerre d'indépendance des États-Unis (1775-1782). Il termine sa carrière militaire avec la dignité de marécha
2 CONC l de France.
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean-Baptiste_Donatien_de_Vimeur,_comte_de_Rochambeau Marshal of France Jean-Baptiste Donatien de Vimeur, Comte de Rochambeau] (French pronunciation: ​[ʁɔʃɑ̃bo]; 1 July 1725 – 10 May 1807) was a Fre
2 CONC nch nobleman and general who played a major role in helping America win independence during the American Revolution. During this time, he served as commander-in-chief of the French Expeditionary Force which embarked from France in
2 CONC  order to help the American Continental Army fight against British forces.
2 CONT
2 CONT Military life
2 CONT
2 CONT Rochambeau was born in Vendôme, in the province of Orléanais (now in the département of Loir-et-Cher). He was schooled at the Jesuit college in Blois. However, after the death of his elder brother, he entered a cavalry regiment, a
2 CONC nd served in Bohemia, Bavaria, and on the Rhine, during the War of the Austrian Succession. In 1747 he had attained the rank of colonel.
2 CONT
2 CONT He took part in the siege of Maastricht in 1748 and became governor of Vendôme in 1749. After distinguishing himself in 1756 in the Battle of Minorca on the outbreak of the Seven Years' War, he was promoted to Brigadier General of
2 CONC  infantry. In 1758, he fought in Germany, notably in the battles of Krefeld and Clostercamp, receiving several wounds during the latter.
2 CONT
2 CONT American Revolution
2 CONT
2 CONT Main article: Franco-American alliance http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franco-American_alliance
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1780, Rochambeau was appointed commander of land forces as part of the project code named Expédition Particulière. He was given the rank of Lieutenant General in command of some 7,000 French troops and sent to join the Continen
2 CONC tal Army, under George Washington in the American Revolutionary War Rochambeau commanded more troops than did Washington. Count Axel von Fersen the Younger served as Rochambeau's aide-de-camp and interpreter. The small size of the
2 CONC  force at his disposal made him initially reluctant to lead the expedition.
2 CONT
2 CONT He landed at Newport, Rhode Island, on 10 July, but was held there inactive for a year, owing to his reluctance to abandon the French fleet blockaded by the British in Narragansett Bay. Brown University, then named the College in 
2 CONC the Colony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations, served as an encampment site for some of Rochambeau's troops, and the College Edifice, now known as University Hall, was converted into a military hospital. In July 1781, Roch
2 CONC ambeau's force left Rhode Island, marching across Connecticut to join Washington on the Hudson River in Mt.Kisco, New York. From July 6 to August 18, 1781, the Odell farm served as Rochambeau's headquarters. There then followed th
2 CONC e celebrated march of the combined forces, the siege of Yorktown and the Battle of the Chesapeake. On 22 September, they combined with Marquis de Lafayette's troops and forced Lord Cornwallis to surrender on 19 October. In recogni
2 CONC tion of his services, the Congress of the Confederation presented him with two cannons taken from the British. These guns, with which Rochambeau returned to Vendôme, were requisitioned in 1792.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was an original member of The Society of the Cincinnati.
2 CONT
2 CONT Return to France
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his return to France, he was honored by King Louis XVI and was made governor of Picardy, a historical province in the north of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the French Revolution, he commanded the Armée du Nord (the military of France) during the period between the fall of the Ancien Régime under Louis XVI in 1792 and the formation of the First French Empire under Napoleon Bona
2 CONC parte in 1804, and created Marshal of France on 28 December 1791, but resigned in 1792 after several reverses. Despite being sympathetic to the National Assembly, Rochambeau was arrested during the Reign of Terror, a period of vio
2 CONC lence that occurred after the onset of the French Revolution, and narrowly escaped the guillotine. He was subsequently pensioned by Napoleon I and died at Thoré-la-Rochette during the First French Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Honors
2 CONT
2 CONT A statue of Rochambeau by Ferdinand Hamar was unveiled in Lafayette Square in Washington, D.C., by President Theodore Roosevelt on 24 May 1902, as a gift from France to the United States. The ceremony was made the occasion of a gr
2 CONC eat demonstration of friendship between the two nations. France was represented by ambassador Jules Cambon, Admiral Fournier and General Henri Brugère, as well as a detachment of sailors and marines from the battleship Gaulois. Re
2 CONC presentatives of the Lafayette and Rochambeau families also attended. A Rochambeau fête was held simultaneously in Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1934, American A. Kingsley Macomber donated a statue of General Rochambeau to the city of Newport, Rhode Island. The sculpture is a replica of a statue in Paris. It was from Newport that General Rochambeau departed with his arm
2 CONC y to join General Washington to march on to the Siege of Yorktown. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The French Navy gave his name to the ironclad frigate Rochambeau.
2 CONT
2 CONT The USS Rochambeau was a transport ship that saw service in the United States Navy during World War II.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Monday, 30 March 2009, U.S. President Barack Obama signed into law the Omnibus Public Land Management Act, one of whose provisions is to designate the Washington-Rochambeau Revolutionary Route as a National Historic Trail.
2 CONT
2 CONT Memoirs
2 CONT
2 CONT Rochambeau's memoirs, Mémoires militaires, historiques et politiques, de Rochambeau were published by Jean-Charles-Julien Luce de Lancival in 1809. Of the first volume, a part that was translated by M.W.E. Wright into the English 
2 CONC Language was published in 1838 under the title of Memoirs of the Marshal Count de R. relative to the War of Independence in the United States.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rochambeau's correspondences during the American campaign were published in H. Doniol's Histoire de la participation de la France en l'établissement des Etats Unis d'Amérique, or History of French Participation in the Establishmen
2 CONC t of the United States, in 1892; (MLA citation, Doniol, H. Histoire de la participation de la France en l'établissement des Etats Unis d'Amérique, Vol. V. [publisher unknown] Paris: 1892.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Miscellany
2 CONT
2 CONT Rochambeau's son, Donatien-Marie-Joseph de Vimeur was an important figure in the French Revolutionnary and Napoleonic Wars.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Rochambeau Middle School in Southbury, Connecticut is named for the comte de Rochambeau, as is the Rochambeau Bridge which carries Interstate 84 and U.S. Highway 6 between Southbury and Newtown, Connecticut (Rochambeau's army mar
2 CONC ched through the area during the American Revolutionary War). There are also various shopping centers and minor streets named in Rochambeau's honor throughout Connecticut.
2 CONT
2 CONT  The French international school (lycée français) in Bethesda, Maryland is named Lycée Rochambeau.
2 CONT
2 CONT  A bridge over the Potomac River in Washington, D.C., is also named for Rochambeau.
2 CONT
2 CONT  There is a Rochambeau Drive named in his honor in Greenburgh, New York and in Williamsburg, Virginia, which is not far from the Yorktown battlefield.
2 CONT
2 CONT  There is a Rochambeau Avenue named in his honor in Providence, Rhode Island, as well as a Rochambeau Street in both New Bedford, Massachusetts and Dobbs Ferry, New York.
2 CONT
2 CONT  There is a Rochambeau Avenue named in his honor in the Bronx, New York.
2 CONT
2 CONT  There is a Rochambeau Place in Springfield, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT  There is a statue of Rochambeau in Newport, Rhode Island and another in Washington, D.C. on Pennsylvania Avenue across from the White House in Lafayette Park that, according to the United States Park Service was sculpted by Ferna
2 CONC nd Hamar and cast by the Pal d'Osne foundry in France and dedicated 24 May 1904, and a statue memorializing his meeting with George Washington in Dobbs Ferry, New York.
2 CONT
2 CONT  There is a Rochambeau Playground in the Richmond District in San Francisco, California.
2 CONT
2 CONT  There is a "Rochambeau Farm" on the Historic Guard Hill in Bedford Corners, New York.
2 CONT
2 CONT  There is a Rochambeau monument at French Hill in Marion, Connecticut close to the Asa Barnes Tavern, the eighth campsite of his troops through Connecticut in 1781.
