1 OCCU Businessman, member Continental Army and had distinguished career - on DAR Honour Role, purchased old mill - Bertolet Mill in family for 65 years
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Samuel Bertolet was born on Sept. 14, 1743 in Oley, PA.  He married in 1771 to Ester Frey, his first cousin.  Ester was born August 26, 1756 in Frederick Township, Montgomery County, PA.  She was the daughter of Ja
2 CONC cob Frey and Susanna Bertolet (daughter of Jean and Susanna Harcourt).  Samuel and Ester had eight children together.  Their first child was born and died on the same day, Aug. 11, 1772, is was a son.  The first surviving child wa
2 CONC s Abraham F. Bertolet.  He was born on Aug. 26, 1773.  Jacob was next on March 16, 1776, the first daughter was born on Dec. 3rd., 1776.  She lived 33 days, dying on Jan. 5, 1777.  Samuel Bertolet was born May 4, 1779, Benjamin on
2 CONC  Dec. 10, 1783, Elizabeth on April 25, 1785 and lastly Daniel Bertolet on Jan. 3, 1787.
2 CONT
2 CONT The story goes that his uncle, Jacob Frey, passed away leaving a widow and children with a home that was only partially built.  Samuel had been living on his father's farm in Oley until then.  Upon the death of Jacob Frey, his wid
2 CONC ow called him to her assistance, on the farm at Frederick.  Samuel Bertolet lived with the widow Frey one year, when he married her oldest daughter, Ester.  Jacob Frey's new homestead was a two story brick house and Samuel Bertole
2 CONC t assisted Mrs. Frey to the home's completion.  In the western gable is a stone marker - J.F. 1770.  On the front, over the middle window, we find a brown stone marked S.B. 1771.  This house is known as the Frey and Bertolet's hom
2 CONC estead.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ester died on June 12, 1887 leaving Samuel to care for her family of little ones.  Three years later on Jan. 26, 1790, Samuel married Ester's younger sister, Elizabeth Frey.  They would start a new family to add to the one Ester h
2 CONC ad left behind.  Samuel and Elizabeth would have five children together: John, Susanna, Daniel, Ester and Elizabeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel was a prosperous businessman and was extremely influential in his neighborhood.  He led an earnest, exemplary, Christian life, and was a deep student of the bible.  He fostered religion and was an intelligent observer of ev
2 CONC ents.  When the call came that his country needed him, he joined the Continental Army.  In his enlistment his name is printed and Samuel Bartly.  The name under the French pronunciation is given thus: Bar-to-la: the "e" takes the 
2 CONC sound of an "a" sound and the "t" is silent.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the spring of 1777 all young men of proper age to serve the Pennsylvania Militia were called, to assemble in each township, at their division headquarters, and especially so in the eastern counties when General Howe was pre
2 CONC paring to invade the state of Pennsylvania.
2 CONT
2 CONT May 6, 1777, Frederick Antes was appointed Colonel of the 6th Battalion of the Pennsylvania County Militia; his regiment was composed of eight companies from the following townships: Douglass, New Hanover, Upper Hanover, Frederick
2 CONC , Limerick, Marborough, Perkiomen and Salford.  Frederick Weiss was appointed Lieutenant and Pay Master, Michael Dotterer was appointed Captain of Fredrick Township Militia; he received his commission May 12, 1777, and served in t
2 CONC hat capacity with the Frederick Township Militia for that year.
2 CONT
2 CONT The company was composed of the following, as found in the PA. Archives: Peter Acker, Francis Bart, Jacob Belts, Samuel Bartly (Bertolet), Conrad Bickard, Henry Boyer, Jacob Bauer, Philip Boyer, Jacob Christman, Jacob Detweiler, e
2 CONC tc.  The regiment, after it was fully formed and organized, proceeded to Newton, Bucks County, PA. near Trenton on the west bank of the Delaware River.  From this point, the regiment followed the army to Philadelphia, where Washin
2 CONC gton established his camp on the hillside, now Falls-of-Schuylkill. From this point Washington's Army traveled to the Brandywine on the 11th of Sept., 1777. During the battle the militia including Samuel Bertolet's regiment had ch
2 CONC arge of the stores on the Chester Road.  Washington's Army sustained a defeat and retreated to Chester that night, and the next day went back to the old camp at Falls-of-Schuylkill.  Two days later part of the army had another eng
2 CONC agement at Busks Tavern, where some of the militia crossed the Schuylkill at Parkersford, PA.  General Washington arrived in camp with his army on Sept. 21, 1777.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Oct. 4, 1777, the campaign was ended, and the militia was discharged for the year.  General Wayne had orders to remain in Chester County with part of his brigade and some of the militia to guard and protect the army while cross
2 CONC ing the Schuylkill at Parkersford, Sept. 19, 1777.  General Muhlenberg says the army was we breast-high in crossing the Schuylkill River; that same night the British attacked General Wayne by surprise at Paole, where he suffered a
2 CONC  severe defeat and a large number of the soldiers were killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Bertolet's teams were in this engagement.  The surprise was so sudden that they had to fly in every direction and from here his teams hauled the wounded soldiers to the Swamp Reformed Church Hospital.  After this defeat the
2 CONC  army went into their headquarters in Samuel Bertolet's barn on his farm.  This barn was built by William Frey in 1738.  Mrs. Elizabeth Bertolet Frey used to say that "the barn was full of horses, even the threshing floor, and the
2 CONC y fed the grain in the sheaves to the horses while the army was encamped there."
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Bertolet continued on the Frey farm during the winter of 1777 and 1778 furnishing supplies and grain to the army at Valley Forge.  General Washington made a special trip during the cold and bleak winter of 1777 and 1778 to 
2 CONC Falchner Swamp.  Samuel Bertolet and Frederick Antes were urged by him, personally, to send more stores and provisions to the camp at Valley Forge, and on this occasion Washington remained some eight or ten days with Colonel Antes
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT On Dec. 31, 1789, Samuel Bertolet purchased from Adam Wartman, forty-five acres of land in New Hanover Township, including what later became know as the old Bertolet Mill.  The old property included the water rights, and was locat
2 CONC ed along the banks of the Swamp Creek and along the road leading from the Frey-Bertolet homestead.  The original mill and dam were built in 1733 by Henry Antes, the father of Colonel Frederick Antes.  When Samuel Bertolet came int
2 CONC o possession of the mill property it was located near the dam along the road.  This location was not pleasing to the new owner as the spring floods and ice would surround his mill, so he dug the head race along the hillside about 
2 CONC 500 yards to the present location of the mill, where it was rebuilt in 1803.  This mill was in the Bertolet family for 65 years.  The old mill ground the grain for the Revolutionary Army while Samuel Bertolet was serving in the ar
2 CONC my and while they were encamped at Camp Pottsgrove.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Bertolet was a man who took great pride in his sons and provided each of them with a home as they grew to maturity.  To his oldest son Abraham, he assigned the mill property.  For his son Jacob he bought a farm in Limerick 
2 CONC Township, near in the Schuylkill Valley.  For his some Samuel he bought the mill property on the French Creek by Pughtown, PA.  When he made his will he never mentioned these three sons, as he had already provided fro them.  In hi
2 CONC s will he only speaks of his two sons, John and Daniel, between whom he divided his 200 acre farm.  To John he gave the homestead, with 100 acres of land.  He gave to Daniel 100 acres of unimproved land.  He reserved from the land
2 CONC  he gave Daniel 80 perches as a burying ground for his family.  This burying ground was incoporated April 21, 189-, by his descendants, as the Bertolet's Burying Ground Association of Frederick Township, Montgomery County, PA.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Daughters of the American Revolution have Samuel Bertolet's name in their book of honour.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT BERTOLET, SAMUEL  
