1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=40839555
2 CONT
2 CONT "In Memory of Mr. David Campbell who died Nov 11, 1769 in ye 58th Year of His Age."
2 CONT
2 CONT David Hugh Campbell was the son of Hugh Campbell (b. 1685, d.1760) and Mary Patterson (b.1684, d.1728). His parents were both born in Scotland but died in Mansfield, Tolland Co., Connecticut. He had a younger brother named Andrew 
2 CONC who was also born in Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT David's parents were married on 21 Feb 1706 in Galston, Scotland. When his mother died in 1728 his father remarried on 18 Apr 1728 to Abigail Mirch (b.1700, d.1754) in Mansfield, Tolland Co., Connecticut.
2 CONT
2 CONT David was a farmer and the owner operator of a Grist Mill and a Saw Mill. He He declared as property one female household slave named "Dinah" which he left to his wife in his will. When he died his farm, home, barns, mills, etc. w
2 CONC ere left to his eldest son David Campbell.
2 CONT
2 CONT David married Janet Robertson during 1732 in Blandford, Hampden Co., MA. Janet lived her entire life in Blandford being born during 1712 and passing in 1777.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT On passenger lists to America 1735 at age 24. David Campbell first appeared in Blandford, Massachusetts (then known as Glasgow) in 1739 when he purchased 60 acres of land there. He remained in Blandford for 30 years until his deat
2 CONC h on 11 Nov 1769. vid, Margaret, Nancy, and Jennet.ford Scouts" by Sumner Gilbert Wood suggests that David Campbell came to Blandford from Pemaquid (Bristol, Maine), but this was proven to be incorrect. 11, 1769 in ye 58th Year of
2 CONC  His Age."ld, Tolland Co., Connecticut. He had a younger brother named Andrew who was also born in Scotland and a second that was killed by Native Americans. ton, Scotland. When his mother died in 1728 his father remarried on 18 A
2 CONC pr 1728 to Abigail Mirch (b.1700, d.1754) in Mansfield, Tolland Co., Connecticut.a Grist Mill and a Saw Mill. He first appeared in Blandford Town records in 1739, when he bought lot 20 from Samuel Cook. He was a pew holder in the 
2 CONC Presbyterian Church and a voter in 1760. He declared as property one female household slave named "Dinah" which he left to his wife in his will. He stated: "I give and bequeath to my wife Genat my Negro Garl to be at her disposing
2 CONC ....". When he died his farm, home, barns, mills, etc. were left to his eldest son David Campbell.orn during 1712 and passing in 1777." (Find a Grave)Campbell or the brother of Andrew Campbell in Mansfield, Connecticut. There is n
