1 NAME James James /Grant/
2 GIVN James James
2 SURN Grant
2 _MARNM Grant
1 NAME James GRANT, Laird of Ballindalloch
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Ballindalloch, Moray, Scotland
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1720
2 PLAC Estate of Ballindalloch, Banff, Banffshire, Scotland
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 13 APR 1806
2 PLAC Ballindalloch, Moray, Scotland
2 ADDR
1 OCCU Governor of East Florida
1 TITL Major General
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Grant_(British_Army_officer,_born_1720)
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 	1720 Ballindalloch, Scotland Died 	13 April 1806 (aged 85–86)
2 CONT
2 CONT Allegiance 	 Kingdom of Great Britain
2 CONT
2 CONT Service/branch 	 British Army Years of service 	1744–1805 Rank 	Major General
2 CONT
2 CONT Commands held 	77th Regiment of Foot (1757–1763) 55th Regiment of Foot (1775–1791) 11th Regiment of Foot (1791–1796)
2 CONT
2 CONT Battles/wars 	
2 CONT
2 CONT War of the Austrian Succession: French and Indian War, Battle of Fort Duquesne, Anglo-Cherokee War, Siege of Havana
2 CONT
2 CONT American War of Independence: Battle of Bunker Hill, New York Campaign (Battle of Brooklyn, Battle of White Plains, Battle of Long Island, Battle of Brandywine Creek), Battle of Barren Hill
2 CONT
2 CONT Battle of La Vigie
2 CONT
2 CONT Other work 	
2 CONT
2 CONT     Governor, East Florida (1763–1771)
2 CONT     MP for Tain Burghs (1773–80); MP for Sutherland (1787–1802)
2 CONT     Governor, Stirling Castle (1789-?)
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.drbronsontours.com/Bronsongrantoutline.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Outline of Governor Colonel James Grant First British Governor of St. Augustine 1720 - Born at Ballindalloch Castle in the Scotish Highlands. He was of the clan of Strathspey.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1737 - College in Edinburgh reading for law
2 CONT
2 CONT 1741 - Joins the British Army after studying law as Ensign in the Royal Scots at the age of twenty-two.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1744 -  Promoted to first lieutenant in Captain John Cunninghams grenadier company of Royal         Scots.
2 CONT      October 24 became Captain in the 1st Battalion, 1st Royal Scots promoted by Field Marshall George Wade.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1745 - Fought at the Battle of Fontenoy
2 CONT
2 CONT 1746 -  (From Captain James Grant to Robert Grant
2 CONT        of Tanmore)
2 CONT            From on Board his Majestyâ€™s Ship Superbe
2 CONT            June 24th 1746 at Sea three leagues from
2 CONT           St. Helier
2 CONT           Dear Tanmore
2 CONT          . . . . . . the Fleet is now under sail with a fair wind I hope
2 CONT          shall have a favorable passage and shall be sorry not
2 CONT          have another opportunity of writing to you till we arrive
2 CONT          some part of America. . . . .
2 CONT          James Grant
2 CONT
2 CONT         Turned around after a few days and reported to Ireland
2 CONT
2 CONT 1746 - October raided Brittany as part of a six battalion raiding force.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1747 - He was appointed aide to General James St. Clair, Ambassador to the Courts of Vienna and Turin.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1752 - An inventory of clothing belonging to Captain James Grant included: 1 light textured Coat n Gold lace, 1 velvet coat, 1 black coat, 2 light colored plain coats, 1 shirt regimental, 1 damask silk waist with gold lace, 1 blue
2 CONC  silk waist with gold lace, 1 white satin waist embroidered with gold1 pair scarlet breeches, 3 pair black velvet, 2 pair black silk, 1 pair light colored, 3 pair worsted stockings, 1 pair black silk, 2 pair white silk, plain shir
2 CONC ts, 10 socks, 20 handkerchiefs, 3 night caps, 1 sash, 1 pair boots, 1 black silk bad, 1 writing case, 1 strap for razors, 1 case with 6 razors and sizzors, 3 swords and two belts, 1 pair pistols, 1 St. Andrews cross.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1757 - On January 5, he was commissioned Major of the 77th Regiment, 1st Battalion known as Montgomery Highlanders by King George the Second, commanded by Archibald Montgomery. They sailed from Cork, Ireland and arrived at Halifax
2 CONC  in August. September 29th he arrived in Charleston, South Carolina.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1758 - The regiment arrived in Philadelphia and were reviewed by General Forbes. In September Major Grant, with eight hundred men including Virginians under Captain Bullitt reconnoitred Fort Duquesne. Grant (without orders) took p
2 CONC ost on a hill near the fort, and dividing his force, tried to draw the garrison out into an ambush. Care was taken to distinguish the British troops by putting a white shirt over their clothes. Disaster for the British troops. He 
2 CONC lost 1/3 of his troops in a battle that ranks as one of the 20 great Native American victories. He was captured with nineteen of his officers. When captured he sent a letter explaining that the responsibility for the defeat was wi
2 CONC th Virginian Major Andrew Lewis. The letter was captured and brought to Major Lewis who was also a captive at Fort Pitt. Major Lewis challenged Grant to a duel which Grant refused. Major Lewis then spat upon Grant. Grant would be 
2 CONC a captive in Quebec for a year.
2 CONT
2 CONT This is summed up by the noted 19th century historian John Fisk in his book New France and New England as:  "Now there was in the English army a hot-headed and ill-balanced Scotch officer named James Grant. He was a supercilious s
2 CONC ort of person, and looked down with ineffable contempt upon the provincial troops. It was very irksome to Major Grant to be within fifty miles of Fort Duquesne and not engage in some kind of work more exciting than that of spade a
2 CONC nd pickaxe; so he sought and obtained permission from Bouquet to take a thousand men and go forward to reconnoitre the situation. Grant went forward, but did not return until he had provoked a fight with the enemy, in which he was
2 CONC  ignominiously defeated with a loss of one quarter of his force. This Grant was afterward a member of Parliament, and served in the British army during a large part of the Revolutionary War. He is now perhaps best remembered for a
2 CONC  remark which he made in the House of Commons in 1774, to the effect that the Americans were an undisciplined rabble who would take to their heels at the first sound of a cannon. But two years after that unlucky speech, when he me
2 CONC t Smallwoodâ €™s Marylanders at the battle of Long Island and pounded them four hours without making them give up an inch of ground, he found reason to amend his opinion. "
2 CONT
2 CONT His nephew (William Grant) congratulated him upon his escape at Fort Duquesne and told him that everyone in Scotland approved of his conduct and courage but were sorry that the success was not equal to the bravery of the attempt.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1759 November 15 - exchanged at Crown Point. Made a full report of conversations with French officers to Lord Amhurst that was used in campaign of 1760.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1760 - He was appointed Lieutenant-Colonel of the 40th Foot.  His correspondence from   General Jeffery Amherst indicated that in the campaign against the Cherokees he should "not think of coming away till you have most effectival
2 CONC ly punished these scoundreal Indians, as without, that, it will be ever to begin again. As to treating with them, it will be time enough when they are so low that you may be sure they cannot hurt the Province again soon, and even 
2 CONC then, that is a business which you will leave to the legislature of the Province, as my intentions are not to interfere in their treaties."
2 CONT
2 CONT 1761-62  - He arrived in South Carolina with five large transports from New York under the convoy of the Nightinggale commanded by Captain Campbell. Commanded British regulars and Carolina militia in South Carolina against Cheroke
2 CONC es. Attakullakulla, the Little Carpenter, gave Grant the name "the Corn Puller" for his part in this campaign. Grant commanded a British force of over 2,800 soldiers. They burned the Cherokee towns at Cowee and Tuckaseegee. His ar
2 CONC my included Carolina Provincials, a battalion of Royal Scotts, 400 rangers, 240 wagoneers, a score of Catawba and Chickasaw scouts, six Mohawk warriors   and 81 black slaves. On June 10 a battle took place before Cowee Mountain. A
2 CONC fter the battle they destroyed the towns of Stickoee, Kithuwa, Tuckareetchee and Tesuntee. Lt. Francis Marion (the Swamp Fox of the American Revolution) reported: "We proceeded, by Col. Grant's orders, to burn the Indian cabins. S
2 CONC ome of the men seemed to enjoy this cruel work, laughing heartily at the curling flames, but to me appeared a shocking sight. But when we came, according to orders, to cut down the fields of corn, I could scarcely refrain from tea
2 CONC rs. Who, without grief, could see...the staff of life sink under our swords with all their precious load, to wither and rot untasted in their mourning fields?"  Andrew Pickens a South Carolina officer called it "british cruelty wh
2 CONC ich I always abhorred." Another officer who served with Col Grant was William Moultrie of Revolutionary War fame and brother of John Moultrie, the soon to be Lieut Governor of Florida. After the action against the Cherokees he was
2 CONC  sent to take possession of the Island of St. Lucia. He would return to this place during the Revolutionary War. (For newspaper dispaches of the campaign)
2 CONT
2 CONT Journal Excerpt "12th and 13th. We halted. The corn by the Town was destroyed, partys were sent out to burn the scattered houses and to pull up beans, peas and corn and to demolish every eatable thing in the country. The Indians w
2 CONC ith one of our partys destroyed the new Towns of Neowee and Camonga, one of them settled by the people who formerly lived at Etchoe, the other by some of the inhabitants of the lower towns.
2 CONT
2 CONT A scout of our Indians killed a Cherokee and wounded another at Ayoree ; a poor miserable squaw was brought in from Tassee and put to death in the Indian camp by one of the Catawbaws.
2 CONT
2 CONT 14th. The Lower Chickesaws, about 20, tired of the service or thinking we should get into a scrape, declined going further and went down the country. We marched to Wattagui, destroyed that Town and the corn about it and so proceed
2 CONC ed to Ayoree.
2 CONT
2 CONT 15th. We halted and sent partys to burn and destray all the plantations which could be found in the country.
2 CONT
2 CONT The troops were reduced to twelve ounces of flour that we might be able to remain in the Indian country long"
2 CONT
2 CONT 1763 Participated in the Siege of Havana, Cuba June 7th to August 16. Appointed Lieutenant Governor.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1763 - March 17 - appointed the first colonial governor of British East Florida
2 CONT
2 CONT 1763 - July 30 - sends first installment of plan to Board of Trade on how to settle new colonies in America
2 CONT
2 CONT 1763 - September 13 - encourages the Board of Trade to set the northern border of Florida at the St. Mary's river.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1763 - September 28 - Board sets border for Florida at the St. Mary's River
2 CONT
2 CONT 1764 - August - arrives in St. Augustine with 3 French chefs.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1764 - October 31 - Grant inaugurated as St. Augustine's (East Florida's) Governor. Inaugurated James Moultrie as Chief justice, appointed John Moultrie to be President of Council of East Florida.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1766 - July 4 - Given authority by the Board of Trade to purchase slaves to do public work.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1767 - In June he was elected a Governor of the Charity of the Magdalen House in London. He was presented with a staff and a Book of Rules and Orders for the society. This was part of an attempt to bring these ("They are not Virgi
2 CONC ns, tis true.") women to the East Florida colony as servants.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1770 - July Inherited title to Ballindalloch Castle following the deaths of his elder brother Alexander Grant and Alexander's son, William
2 CONT
2 CONT 1771 - May 9  - ill with a stomach disorder left Florida.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1771 - January 25 - official termination of Office of Governor of Florida
2 CONT
2 CONT 1772 He was appointed Brevet-Colonel
2 CONT
2 CONT 1773 - April - resigns as Governor takes a seat in the House of Commons for Wickburghs and at the general election in 1774 for Sutherlandshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1775 -  February 2 - Rose in the House of Commons and stated: Americans "could not fight... (he would) undertake to march from one end of the continent to the other with five thousand men."
2 CONT
2 CONT December appointed Colonel of the 55th Foot.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1776 - Returns to America as a Brigadier  in the British Army - Boston. He occupies John Hancock's house.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1776 - August - Commander of the west wing of the British army at the Battle of Long Island.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1776 - December - Theater commander at Trenton and Princeton. He had sent word to the Hessian commander, Johann Rahl that Washington intended to attack but the information arrived on December 25 at 5:00 pm and no action was taken 
2 CONC by Rahl..
2 CONT
2 CONT 1777 - Becomes a Major-General. Commands the right wing at the battles of Brandywine and Germantown.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1778 - May 20 - fails to trap LaFayette at Barren Hill.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1778 - October 27 leads a successful expeditionary force to capture the French West Indian island of St. Lucia.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1780 - defeated in parlimentary elections
2 CONT
2 CONT 1782 - Appointed Lieutenant General General.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1787 - Reelected to Parliment
2 CONT
2 CONT 1789 - Governor of Stirling Castle and commanding general of Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT 1791 - Transfered from the 55th to the 11th Foot.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1796 - Appointed General and retired from active military services
2 CONT
2 CONT 1802 - Retires to estate on the Avon and Spey rivers as the Laird of Ballindalloch after relinguishing seat in Parliment..
2 CONT
2 CONT 1805 - Retires from the British army
2 CONT
2 CONT 1806 - April 13 Dies at 86. Estate goes to grandnephew, George Macpherson.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
