1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ALEXANDER1 MCDANNALD was born 1715 in Aberdeen Scotland, and died 1783 in Culpeper County, Virginia. He married ISOBELL MCLAUGHLAN December 12, 1720 in West or Old Parish, Greenock, Renfrew, Scotland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for ALEXANDER MCDANNALD:
2 CONT
2 CONT  Notes from Shelley Erickson:
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexander fought for Charles Stuart at Prestonpas and Culloden. see http://www.scotshistoryonline.co.uk/charlieb.html
2 CONT
2 CONT He was deported to VA after Culloden 1746. Settled in Orange County, Virginia near Cedar Mountain. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ranson McBride- "Lists of Scottish Rebel Prisoners Transported to America in the aftermath of Culloden 1746" lists Alexander McDonald.
2 CONT
2 CONT =See:  https://www.electricscotland.com/webclans/stoz/stewarth5.htm for the manifest of the''' Gildart'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT and
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://web.archive.org/web/20020830131115/http://ftp.rootsweb.com/pub/usgenweb/md/statewide/ships/gildart.txt
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT He signed a will on January 29, 1783 in St. Mark's Parish, Culpeper county, Virginia. Children mentioned in the will:
2 CONT
2 CONT John, Rueban, George, Mary Turner, Sarah Drisdle, Nelley, Elizabeth Turner, Peggy Shropshire and grandson Roddy Turner, son of Jeremiah Turner. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He had an estate probated on February 17, 1783 in St. Mark's Parish. Executors: Captain John Majors and Thomas Pourter. He was born in Aberdeen, Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Scots were a choice few of 88 convicts of the Battle of Culloden. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They came to America on the ship Gildart, which was just one of many ships that carried the families that were banished for some reason or other. Transported on the Gildart, master Richard Holmes, from Liverpool, 24 February 1747 
2 CONC to Port North Potomac Maryland..August 5, 1747. On the list:
2 CONT
2 CONT MCDonald, Alexander
2 CONT
2 CONT http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~nightwolf/Descendants%20of%20Alexander%20McDannald.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT http://familytreemaker.genealogy.com/users/w/a/l/Leila-Walsh-IA/PDFBOOK1.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT Long discussion":  http://boards.ancestry.co.uk/localities.northam.usa.states.virginia.counties.culpeper/4521/mb.ashx?pnt=1
2 CONT
2 CONT More About ALEXANDER MCDANNALD and ISABELLA MCLAUGHLIN:
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage: December 12, 1732, West or Old Parish, Greenrock, Renfrew, Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of ALEXANDER MCDANNALD and ISABELLA MCLAUGHLIN are:
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. i. JOHN2 MCDANNALD, b. 1733, Scotland; d. Bet. 1812 - 1813, Culpeper County, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. ii. GEORGE WARREN MCDANNALD, b. 1760, Culpeper County, Virginia; d. July 05, 1819, Fleming County, Kentucky.
2 CONT
2 CONT iii. SARAH MCDANNALD, m. MR. DRISDLE.
2 CONT
2 CONT iv. ELIZABETH MCDANNALD.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. v. MARY MCDANNALD.
2 CONT
2 CONT vi. PEGGY MCDANNALD, m. MR. SHROPSHIRE.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. vii. REUBEN MCDANNALD, b. February 14, 1768, Montgomery County, Kentucky (possibly Culpeper County, Virginia); d. August 14, 1854, Paynesville, Pike County, Missouri.
2 CONT
2 CONT viii. MOLLY MCDANNALD.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Fifth Jacobite Uprising (1745)
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Edward Stuart, the son of James VIII, aka The Young Pretender and Bonnie Prince Charlie, landed in Scotland in July 1745. The English, under General John Cope ( of the song Johnny Cope) moved north, but not knowing the siz
2 CONC e of Jacobite forces, avoided battle. He marched to Inverness and Aberdeen and then finally, in September sailed to Edinburgh to meet the Jacobite forces that were at Dunbar. The Battle of Presonpans was a complete victory for the
2 CONC  Jacobites (largely due to the efforts of Lord George Murray). The total number of men involved was only around 2500 - and the battle was over almost as soon as it began. Cope's troops broke rans and fled.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Jacobites got to within 130 miles of London, but at Derby fell to fighting amongst each other. Without support from the Scottish lowlands or England, and with a promised French force never materializing, they were forced to re
2 CONC treat. Murray led a skilful retreat from Derby and defeated the English at Falkirk in January 1747. He opposed Charles Edward Stuart's decision to stand at Culloden because of the terrain. Nevertheless, the Jacobites took the stan
2 CONC d. At Culloden Moor they met the army of the Duke of Cumberland (King George II's son and known to Scots as "Butcher Cumberland"). The Scots were cut down by cannonfire and the exhausted few that made it to English lines were cut 
2 CONC down.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bonnie Prince Charlie was hounded for months by English troops. The Butcher of Cumberland brutally suppressed the Highlands. Wearing of the kilt and use of the tartan was prohibited on pain of death, the gathering of clans was for
2 CONC bidden and the Highland culture virtually destroyed. Bonnie Prince Charlie escaped only with the help of Flora MacDonald, who dressed him up as her servant "Betty Burke" to get him safely to the Isle of Skye where he then took shi
2 CONC p to France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bonnie Prince Charlie's fortunes never recovered. He became an alocholic. His wife eventually deserted him and he moved to Rome where he died in 1788 at the age of 67. His brother, Henry Stuart, Cardinal York became the last Stuar
2 CONC t pretender. The Jacobite was lost, alive only in sentiment and in a large number of Jacobite songs. The cause eventually became so romanticized that George III even granted a pension to Henry Stuart. "  http://www.contemplator.co
2 CONC m/history/jacobite.html
2 CONT
2 CONT http://familytreemaker.genealogy.com/users/h/a/r/Dana-Harp/GENE4-0001.html
