0 @N6000000003087580007@ NOTE Sources:
1 CONT
1 CONT Histoire a Genealogie des Acadiens Grand-Pre in a footnote on p. 624:
1 CONT In the 1678 census of Port Royal there is listed "Marie Sale, widow of Jean Claude." 
1 CONT
1 CONT Also, in the 1686 census will be found, "Marie Sale." 
1 CONT
1 CONT Don Landry, Historian:
1 CONT According to both Stephen White and Father Clarence J. d'Entremont,  Jehan (or Jean, or John) Claude was an Indian, of the MicMac tribe.  His name occurs twice in the Port Royal Church Registers, ALWAYS as Jehan Clause.
1 CONT
1 CONT Whit Dartez, of Houston. TX:
1 CONT Clarence J. d’Entremont” in his book "Histoire du Cap-Sable”" (vol-3 page 1124) says that in a rare case where a European woman marries an Amerindian, considered by Europeans as an inferior race. He is saying that Marie Sale was m
1 CONC arried to Jean Claude a micmaque.
1 CONT Plus he goes on to say that Genevieve Massignon has toyed with the idea that Martin Aucoin, the ancestor of all the Aucoins in Acadia, could be the son of Marie Sale in a first marriage with Martin Aucoin, the 20 Jan 1632 at La Ro
1 CONC chelle, France.
